WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.091
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4622.

2
00:00:01.091 --> 00:00:02.182
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:02.182 --> 00:00:02.545
<v STEPHANIE DRAGOON>Perfect.

4
00:00:02.545 --> 00:00:04.727
There is Velcro on this table.

5
00:00:04.727 --> 00:00:05.091
Alright.

6
00:00:05.091 --> 00:00:06.182
Are we good?

7
00:00:06.182 --> 00:00:07.273
Are we recording?

8
00:00:07.273 --> 00:00:08.000
Oh, perfect.

9
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:08.364
Okay.

10
00:00:08.364 --> 00:00:10.182
Today is May eleventh, 2016.

11
00:00:10.182 --> 00:00:12.727
We are at the Imperial Calcasieu Museum.

12
00:00:12.727 --> 00:00:24.727
I'm Stephanie Dragoon with the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries and I'm interviewing today Ms. Brenda Cole Jones to talk about her memories of Mossville,

13
00:00:24.727 --> 00:00:32.364
Louisiana as a part of a project conducted in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum to document the history of Mossville.

14
00:00:32.364 --> 00:00:35.273
Thank you so much for your time today.

15
00:00:35.273 --> 00:00:36.000
<v BRENDA COLE JONES>Sure.

16
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.830
<v DRAGOON>And for sharing your stories with us.

17
00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:41.660
This is our first interview session together.

18
00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:46.106
I'm going to start with some basic biography kind of questions.

19
00:00:46.106 --> 00:00:46.511
<v JONES>Sure.

20
00:00:46.511 --> 00:00:50.553
<v DRAGOON>Can you please state your full name for the recording?

21
00:00:50.553 --> 00:00:50.957
<v JONES>Sure.

22
00:00:50.957 --> 00:00:52.170
Brenda Cole Jones.

23
00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:55.000
<v DRAGOON>When and where were you born?

24
00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:00.762
<v JONES>I was born in Lake Charles, Louisiana at Saint Patrick Hospital.

25
00:01:00.762 --> 00:01:07.048
Then I lived in . . . For a couple of years in Sulphur [Louisiana].

26
00:01:07.048 --> 00:01:15.429
Then my family moved to the Mossville community in like 1956 as my mother told me.

27
00:01:15.429 --> 00:01:24.857
We moved into the Lincoln Heights subdivision, which was located two blocks behind the Mossville School . . . High School.

28
00:01:24.857 --> 00:01:28.000
<v DRAGOON>And what year were you born?

29
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:28.524
<v JONES>1954.

30
00:01:28.524 --> 00:01:32.190
<v DRAGOON>And you said you moved there in . . .?

31
00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:32.714
<v JONES>1956.

32
00:01:32.714 --> 00:01:33.238
<v DRAGOON>1956.

33
00:01:33.238 --> 00:01:35.333
<v JONES>Yeah, 1956 maybe '57.

34
00:01:35.333 --> 00:01:39.000
But I think she said '56.

35
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.785
<v DRAGOON>What are your parents' names?

36
00:01:41.785 --> 00:01:51.253
<v JONES>My parents’ names were Joseph Cole, Senior and Ruthia D. Vincent . . . I'm sorry, Ruthia D. Perkins Cole.

37
00:01:51.253 --> 00:01:55.709
<v DRAGOON>And what did they do for a living?

38
00:01:55.709 --> 00:01:59.051
<v JONES>My father was a truck driver.

39
00:01:59.051 --> 00:02:06.848
My mother stayed at home, and for a short term she was a beautician.

40
00:02:06.848 --> 00:02:08.519
She did hair.

41
00:02:08.519 --> 00:02:09.633
Hair styling.

42
00:02:09.633 --> 00:02:12.975
<v DRAGOON>Did she do that in Mossville?

43
00:02:12.975 --> 00:02:14.646
<v JONES>Yes she did.

44
00:02:14.646 --> 00:02:19.658
She did it in the Mossville community at home.

45
00:02:19.658 --> 00:02:23.000
<v DRAGOON>And what about your grandparents?

46
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:27.583
<v JONES>My grandparents lived over in the Willow Springs area.

47
00:02:27.583 --> 00:02:31.657
They both were I think from that area.

48
00:02:31.657 --> 00:02:37.769
My grandmother was [Leada?] Vincent [Perkins] and my grandfather was James Perkins.

49
00:02:37.769 --> 00:02:41.843
<v DRAGOON>And what did they do for a living?

50
00:02:41.843 --> 00:02:47.444
<v JONES>My grandfather worked for the railroad and my grandmother did housekeeping.

51
00:02:47.444 --> 00:02:51.519
Then she became a stay at home mom.

52
00:02:51.519 --> 00:02:54.065
<v DRAGOON>Do you have any siblings?

53
00:02:54.065 --> 00:02:55.593
<v JONES>Yes I do.

54
00:02:55.593 --> 00:03:03.231
I have, let's see, I have one brother and he lives here in Lake Charles.

55
00:03:03.231 --> 00:03:06.287
<c.p>His name is Joseph Cole, Junior.

56
00:03:06.287 --> 00:03:10.361
I have two sisters, but they're both deceased.

57
00:03:10.361 --> 00:03:13.926
Yvonne Cole Hester and Deirdra Cole Green.

58
00:03:13.926 --> 00:03:18.000
<v DRAGOON>Why did your family move to Mossville?

59
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.481
<v JONES>There was an opportunity for housing there that they could rent.

60
00:03:22.481 --> 00:03:26.963
It was closer to the area that my father worked in.

61
00:03:26.963 --> 00:03:30.222
He was a truck driver over in Sulphur.

62
00:03:30.222 --> 00:03:32.667
He worked for Como and LaFleur.

63
00:03:32.667 --> 00:03:37.556
It was [a cement] step company, and so he drove trucks there.

64
00:03:37.556 --> 00:03:40.407
<v DRAGOON>What is your earliest memory of Mossville?

65
00:03:40.407 --> 00:03:46.926
<v JONES>My earliest memory is that when we . . . As I got to be close to school age,

66
00:03:46.926 --> 00:03:57.519
I remember we started getting more neighbors to come in because for a long time we were the only family on our end of the block.

67
00:03:57.519 --> 00:04:05.259
There were a couple of other families that moved in and as they moved in they all had children.

68
00:04:05.259 --> 00:04:09.333
So that gave me an opportunity to have some playmates.

69
00:04:09.333 --> 00:04:13.815
There were some families that had moved next door to us.

70
00:04:13.815 --> 00:04:19.111
The first family was the Fontenot’s and they moved back to Lake Charles.

71
00:04:19.111 --> 00:04:22.370
And then the Singleton family moved next door.

72
00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:29.296
Then we had the Miles family, the Reado family, and the Mouton that lived by us, so.

73
00:04:29.296 --> 00:04:30.926
Everybody was real close.

74
00:04:30.926 --> 00:04:46.000
And by my mom not working if any of the other mothers had a job they needed to go to then they would leave their kids with her because they knew that she would be at home.

75
00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:53.333
So even though for a long time I was the only child that my mother and father had,

76
00:04:53.333 --> 00:05:01.481
my father had children from a previous marriage, but for a long time it was just me from that marriage.

77
00:05:01.481 --> 00:05:03.111
So I was excited

78
00:05:03.111 --> 00:05:06.370
<c.p>because I always had somebody to play with.

79
00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:08.000
Somebody coming over.

80
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:19.342
We would go occasionally down to the Mossville area we called “across the track” and we'd go to the recreation center there and play on the playground equipment.

81
00:05:19.342 --> 00:05:25.418
But otherwise we just went from house to house playing games . . . Ball, hopscotch, shooting marbles.

82
00:05:25.418 --> 00:05:30.684
I was a girl, but I had my own little sack of marbles.

83
00:05:30.684 --> 00:05:34.329
My dad had taught me how to shoot marbles.

84
00:05:34.329 --> 00:05:37.975
So it was just everybody was real close knit.

85
00:05:37.975 --> 00:05:42.430
If somebody butchered they shared the meat with the other families.

86
00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:46.076
We all got to know each other's family members.

87
00:05:46.076 --> 00:05:54.177
If something was going on with one family everybody would go over and see their family members and vice versa.

88
00:05:54.177 --> 00:05:59.443
They'd come over to our house if my grandmother came over to visit.

89
00:05:59.443 --> 00:06:01.873
So it was just real nice.

90
00:06:01.873 --> 00:06:08.759
It was . . . When there were shade trees we all got to play outside under the shade trees.

91
00:06:08.759 --> 00:06:18.481
It was a community where I can remember at night you didn't have to lock your doors or windows because it was real safe.

92
00:06:18.481 --> 00:06:28.608
So everybody just . . . Once it got dark you went in and you took your bath and settled down for the night and that was it.

93
00:06:28.608 --> 00:06:30.228
You'd hear cars passing.

94
00:06:30.228 --> 00:06:39.544
But we still didn't have a lot of neighbors even into the early sixties, and then the community, it just continued to grow.

95
00:06:39.544 --> 00:06:44.000
There were more and more families that started coming in.

96
00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:49.239
<v DRAGOON>Did you ever know why there were so many people coming into the community?

97
00:06:49.239 --> 00:06:52.233
Did your parents talk about it at all?

98
00:06:52.233 --> 00:06:55.975
<v JONES>No, I never remembered anybody saying . . . Just like I said,

99
00:06:55.975 --> 00:07:02.712
there were more and more houses that were either moved to the neighborhood, or houses that were built.

100
00:07:02.712 --> 00:07:07.202
There were job opportunities over in Sulphur and the Westlake [Louisiana] area.

101
00:07:07.202 --> 00:07:09.074
So the men that were

102
00:07:09.074 --> 00:07:14.313
<c.p>working, it was easy for them to get back and forth to their jobs.

103
00:07:14.313 --> 00:07:18.804
I remember everybody liking the fact that the school was so close.

104
00:07:18.804 --> 00:07:21.049
The children could walk to school.

105
00:07:21.049 --> 00:07:26.288
And back in the early sixties, a lot of children came home for lunch.

106
00:07:26.288 --> 00:07:28.534
They didn't eat at the school.

107
00:07:28.534 --> 00:07:34.521
We were just that close to the school so it was always somebody coming and going.

108
00:07:34.521 --> 00:07:42.006
We lived on the corner, so therefore we got to see everybody as they were coming in, or going out.

109
00:07:42.006 --> 00:07:45.000
<v DRAGOON>How long did you live in Mossville?

110
00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:47.054
<v JONES>I lived there until 1976.

111
00:07:47.054 --> 00:07:50.750
Then I got married, and then I moved away.

112
00:07:50.750 --> 00:07:51.982
Well not far.

113
00:07:51.982 --> 00:07:54.857
I only moved to Lake Charles [laughs].

114
00:07:54.857 --> 00:07:57.732
<v DRAGOON>Did you meet your husband in Mossville?

115
00:07:57.732 --> 00:08:01.429
<v JONES>Yes, his family moved to Mossville in like, 1969.

116
00:08:01.429 --> 00:08:06.357
And we were all friends and we dated, then we got married.

117
00:08:06.357 --> 00:08:13.750
And when we first married we lived with my parents until we bought a house in Lake Charles.

118
00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:21.143
And the only reason we didn't buy one in Mossville, there weren't any houses that were for sale.

119
00:08:21.143 --> 00:08:23.607
Everybody was in their own home.

120
00:08:23.607 --> 00:08:26.893
So everything was basically occupied at that time.

121
00:08:26.893 --> 00:08:34.286
<v DRAGOON>What did Mossville look like when you were really young as opposed to the time that you left?

122
00:08:34.286 --> 00:08:37.982
<v JONES>There were areas where some people would have gardens.

123
00:08:37.982 --> 00:08:39.214
There were areas

124
00:08:39.214 --> 00:08:44.143
<c.p>where you would see people that . . . A couple of people had cattle.

125
00:08:44.143 --> 00:08:53.179
There was families that had lived next door to us . . . Well I call it next door, it was just across the street.

126
00:08:53.179 --> 00:08:59.339
But then Entergy bought that property out and they came through with their power lines.

127
00:08:59.339 --> 00:09:06.732
So those families, they relocated, but it was just a couple of streets away from where we lived.

128
00:09:06.732 --> 00:09:10.018
And like I said, it was . . . green trees.

129
00:09:10.018 --> 00:09:15.357
You always saw somebody out doing laundry . . . Hanging their laundry on the line.

130
00:09:15.357 --> 00:09:19.054
Children getting ready to go to and from school.

131
00:09:19.054 --> 00:09:26.446
So it was a lot of activity that was going on in the area that we lived in.

132
00:09:26.446 --> 00:09:33.839
Then as we grew into teenagers there were still things that we would go to the school for.

133
00:09:33.839 --> 00:09:42.464
Some of the children knew how to ride skateboards, so they would go to the school because there was paved areas.

134
00:09:42.464 --> 00:09:46.571
We didn't have blacktop streets or asphalt streets until 1976.

135
00:09:46.571 --> 00:09:49.036
Everything was rock and dirt roads.

136
00:09:49.036 --> 00:09:53.964
So it was always a lot of dust flying in the area.

137
00:09:53.964 --> 00:09:58.893
We'd get out there and ride our bicycles when we were young.

138
00:09:58.893 --> 00:10:00.946
That didn't stop us any.

139
00:10:00.946 --> 00:10:06.696
But it was, like I said, it was just a lot of fun times.

140
00:10:06.696 --> 00:10:14.089
We knew all the children on our street and the other streets because we went to school together.

141
00:10:14.089 --> 00:10:20.250
Then also on weekends a lot of the families would get together and sit around.

142
00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:26.000
Our mothers would drink coffee and talk and laugh and watch the children.

143
00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:29.008
<v DRAGOON>What do you remember about your family home?

144
00:10:29.008 --> 00:10:33.895
What did it look like and who lived in the house with you?

145
00:10:33.895 --> 00:10:34.271
<v JONES>Okay.

146
00:10:34.271 --> 00:10:39.534
I lived there with my parents, and it was just the three of us.

147
00:10:39.534 --> 00:10:44.797
And then about thirteen . . . Let’s see, I was thirteen when my sister was born.

148
00:10:44.797 --> 00:10:52.316
So for a long time it was just the three of us and then it was the four of us.

149
00:10:52.316 --> 00:10:54.571
We had a wood structure home.

150
00:10:54.571 --> 00:10:58.331
We had a carport on the side for the vehicles.

151
00:10:58.331 --> 00:11:00.962
We had just . . . Regular wood frame home.

152
00:11:00.962 --> 00:11:03.970
It was three bedrooms, so just standard living.

153
00:11:03.970 --> 00:11:05.850
<v DRAGOON>Did you have a garden?

154
00:11:05.850 --> 00:11:06.977
<v JONES>No, we didn't.

155
00:11:06.977 --> 00:11:19.008
We would be in and out because my father was a truck driver and we would go over to my grandmother's just so my mother and I wouldn't be home by ourselves.

156
00:11:19.008 --> 00:11:26.526
Then if they needed transportation to get back and forth to the doctor's or something my mother would be there.

157
00:11:26.526 --> 00:11:32.917
But it was only maybe a fifteen minute drive from our house to where my grandparents were.

158
00:11:32.917 --> 00:11:34.797
<v DRAGOON>So you saw them often?

159
00:11:34.797 --> 00:11:35.549
<v JONES>Oh yes.

160
00:11:35.549 --> 00:11:37.053
Went there every weekend.

161
00:11:37.053 --> 00:11:40.812
Every Sunday I knew where I was going to be.

162
00:11:40.812 --> 00:11:49.083
We were going to be at her house every holiday, and then eventually in the mid-seventies we finally . . . Well the early seventies,

163
00:11:49.083 --> 00:11:59.609
we started to get my grandmother to come and visit us at home and have a meal with us instead of her having to do all the cooking.

164
00:11:59.609 --> 00:12:03.744
But otherwise all the major holidays were spent with my grandmother.

165
00:12:03.744 --> 00:12:06.000
<v DRAGOON>And she cooked a lot?

166
00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:07.465
<v JONES>Oh yeah she cooked.

167
00:12:07.465 --> 00:12:08.564
She loved cooking.

168
00:12:08.564 --> 00:12:11.860
So it was always somebody at my grandmother's house.

169
00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:14.424
And my mother loved to cook, too.

170
00:12:14.424 --> 00:12:15.889
She cooked every day.

171
00:12:15.889 --> 00:12:26.877
It was always a community where I don't care how many children were in a family or how big or small the pot was everybody offered you something to eat.

172
00:12:26.877 --> 00:12:31.272
I used to go, "Why is she offering them something to eat?

173
00:12:31.272 --> 00:12:33.469
We've got only three beans left.

174
00:12:33.469 --> 00:12:34.934
Everybody has already eaten."

175
00:12:34.934 --> 00:12:35.667
The previous

176
00:12:35.667 --> 00:12:37.864
<c.p>people ate so it's nothing left.

177
00:12:37.864 --> 00:12:39.695
But they didn't mind sharing.

178
00:12:39.695 --> 00:12:42.992
It's just a type of community that we had.

179
00:12:42.992 --> 00:12:49.218
I just kind of hated everybody started moving away, but it was just one of the things.

180
00:12:49.218 --> 00:12:52.881
<v DRAGOON>When did you really feel that everyone was moving away?

181
00:12:52.881 --> 00:13:08.630
<v JONES>By the time I got into high school because a lot of the students . . . The children that were in the neighborhood once they graduated if they didn't go to McNeese [University] they had to move out of state looking for jobs and schooling.

182
00:13:08.630 --> 00:13:15.955
So that's when I started noticing a lot of them were moving, but eventually a lot of them came back.

183
00:13:15.955 --> 00:13:19.251
Even if they moved back in with their parents.

184
00:13:19.251 --> 00:13:22.181
So it was a constant in and out.

185
00:13:22.181 --> 00:13:30.971
But I stayed on even after I finished high school and I attended McNeese and I continued to live at home with my family.

186
00:13:30.971 --> 00:13:35.000
<v DRAGOON>And where did you go to elementary and high school?

187
00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:39.855
<v JONES>I went to Mossville from first grade through my freshman year.

188
00:13:39.855 --> 00:13:44.710
Then it was no longer a high school at that time.

189
00:13:44.710 --> 00:13:50.448
If you were ninth through twelfth graders you went to school in Westlake.

190
00:13:50.448 --> 00:13:57.069
Then my sophomore year I started attending Westlake High, and I graduated from Westlake High.

191
00:13:57.069 --> 00:14:05.897
So from 1960 to 1969 I attended Mossville High School, and then from 1969 to 1972 I attended Westlake High.

192
00:14:05.897 --> 00:14:09.869
<v DRAGOON>What do you remember about the teachers at Mossville?

193
00:14:09.869 --> 00:14:11.634
<v JONES>They were very strict.

194
00:14:11.634 --> 00:14:15.607
They wanted you to stay focused on your learnings.

195
00:14:15.607 --> 00:14:16.048
We

196
00:14:16.048 --> 00:14:17.372
<c.p>always had homework.

197
00:14:17.372 --> 00:14:23.993
By the time we got to second and third grade . . . I remember the spelling bees.

198
00:14:23.993 --> 00:14:33.262
I always thought those were nice because everybody wanted to get a certificate and we'd get a list from the teachers.

199
00:14:33.262 --> 00:14:38.559
Everybody would have a list of words to go home and study.

200
00:14:38.559 --> 00:14:42.090
Then you’d get to compete with each other.

201
00:14:42.090 --> 00:14:45.621
We had a band at the high school.

202
00:14:45.621 --> 00:14:47.828
I was in the band.

203
00:14:47.828 --> 00:14:55.772
We had . . . Let's see, we had a home ec [onomics] department where students of course learned to cook.

204
00:14:55.772 --> 00:15:01.069
And I happened to have the same teacher for three different grades.

205
00:15:01.069 --> 00:15:05.041
She taught me in first grade and she left.

206
00:15:05.041 --> 00:15:11.221
Then she came back and then she was my third and fourth grade teacher.

207
00:15:11.221 --> 00:15:14.752
But like I said, the teachers all stayed.

208
00:15:14.752 --> 00:15:19.607
They did have paddles to make sure they got your attention.

209
00:15:19.607 --> 00:15:23.579
They'd hit on that desk and say, "Pay attention."

210
00:15:23.579 --> 00:15:25.345
They got your attention.

211
00:15:25.345 --> 00:15:28.434
I remember saying the pledge of allegiance.

212
00:15:28.434 --> 00:15:32.407
Singing songs every morning when we went to class.

213
00:15:32.407 --> 00:15:37.262
It was just something where they tried to keep you involved.

214
00:15:37.262 --> 00:15:39.910
Keep your attention in the classroom.

215
00:15:39.910 --> 00:15:43.000
<v DRAGOON>What kind of student were you?

216
00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:46.073
<v JONES>I guess I was an honor roll student.

217
00:15:46.073 --> 00:15:49.530
Not guess, but I was an honor roll student.

218
00:15:49.530 --> 00:15:57.213
Always was one of those students I tried to make sure I always had my homework and was always prepared.

219
00:15:57.213 --> 00:16:01.054
Of course my mother played a big role in that.

220
00:16:01.054 --> 00:16:02.590
She was education geared.

221
00:16:02.590 --> 00:16:07.968
She wanted us to not just finish high school, but to further our education.

222
00:16:07.968 --> 00:16:11.810
So she was always making sure we did our homework.

223
00:16:11.810 --> 00:16:19.108
If there were any little school plays or anything, which they always had, she never stopped me from participating.

224
00:16:19.108 --> 00:16:26.790
And she would come to watch us, my sister and I both, whenever we were participating in something at school.

225
00:16:26.790 --> 00:16:30.632
All the way through high school it was the same.

226
00:16:30.632 --> 00:16:35.625
She wouldn't come to a football game to watch us with the marching

227
00:16:35.625 --> 00:16:40.619
<c.p>squad, but she would come because she usually had somebody else with her,

228
00:16:40.619 --> 00:16:45.997
somebody else's child, and they would come and just watch us perform at halftime.

229
00:16:45.997 --> 00:16:48.686
Then she'd leave and go back home.

230
00:16:48.686 --> 00:16:50.990
Come back and pick us up.

231
00:16:50.990 --> 00:16:52.911
But it was always education.

232
00:16:52.911 --> 00:16:58.289
Once you finished high school you needed to try to go a little further.

233
00:16:58.289 --> 00:17:01.746
<v DRAGOON>And you were . . . So you were in the band?

234
00:17:01.746 --> 00:17:03.283
What did you play?

235
00:17:03.283 --> 00:17:04.819
<v JONES>I played a clarinet.

236
00:17:04.819 --> 00:17:11.349
Yeah and when I was like in the sixth grade I had tried out for the majorettes.

237
00:17:11.349 --> 00:17:14.038
I learned how to twirl the baton.

238
00:17:14.038 --> 00:17:16.343
So it was exciting for me.

239
00:17:16.343 --> 00:17:18.648
It gave me something to do.

240
00:17:18.648 --> 00:17:25.562
I was always looking for something else to do when the other kids had gone from the neighborhood.

241
00:17:25.562 --> 00:17:33.629
If anybody went off to visit their grandparents for vacation, or to help out, well then that left me by myself.

242
00:17:33.629 --> 00:17:41.695
So if I found something that would keep me focused my parents didn't have any problem with me participating in it.

243
00:17:41.695 --> 00:17:44.000
<v DRAGOON>Were your parents musical, too?

244
00:17:44.000 --> 00:17:45.589
<v JONES>No, neither one was.

245
00:17:45.589 --> 00:17:51.151
We had one neighbor that she was a real strong Christian lady, Mrs. Miles,

246
00:17:51.151 --> 00:18:00.288
and she would offer to get us all to participate in Bible school in the summer when their church would be having it.

247
00:18:00.288 --> 00:18:06.644
She would always have little wiener roasts at her house and we would go to those.

248
00:18:06.644 --> 00:18:09.027
It was something that was fun.

249
00:18:09.027 --> 00:18:14.986
Back then it wasn't any . . . She wasn't going to let you have any dance music.

250
00:18:14.986 --> 00:18:16.973
It was all gospel music.

251
00:18:16.973 --> 00:18:24.123
You could play the little games, but it all had to be done according to the church music.

252
00:18:24.123 --> 00:18:26.507
But we looked forward to it.

253
00:18:26.507 --> 00:18:29.685
That was all we knew at that time.

254
00:18:29.685 --> 00:18:32.863
We didn't know any other form of music.

255
00:18:32.863 --> 00:18:34.055
So that was

256
00:18:34.055 --> 00:18:39.219
<c.p>a great opportunity to just to get to see each other once again.

257
00:18:39.219 --> 00:18:43.986
It was always somebody shooting basketball at one of the neighbor’s homes.

258
00:18:43.986 --> 00:18:49.548
And the smaller kids, we'd go and sit on the ground and watch them.

259
00:18:49.548 --> 00:18:52.726
The older guys as they would shoot basketball.

260
00:18:52.726 --> 00:18:58.685
Trying to make sure we stayed out the way, but we'd be right there anyway.

261
00:18:58.685 --> 00:19:06.630
<v DRAGOON>What was the major . . . what was . . . What were some of the big differences between your education and your parents' education?

262
00:19:06.630 --> 00:19:09.014
<v JONES>My father was from north Louisiana.

263
00:19:09.014 --> 00:19:14.178
He said he only went to I think maybe third or fourth grade.

264
00:19:14.178 --> 00:19:19.342
So he learned how to write his name, but that was about it.

265
00:19:19.342 --> 00:19:22.521
Whereas my mother, she had finished high school.

266
00:19:22.521 --> 00:19:25.301
She didn't go to college or anything.

267
00:19:25.301 --> 00:19:32.055
She had started working after she graduated from high school and she was still living at home.

268
00:19:32.055 --> 00:19:35.630
And I had the opportunity to go to college.

269
00:19:35.630 --> 00:19:43.973
My sister, she went to McNeese for a while but then she ended up doing to Delta [School of Business College].

270
00:19:43.973 --> 00:19:47.945
So we both had an opportunity to further our education.

271
00:19:47.945 --> 00:19:50.726
We didn't have to pay for it.

272
00:19:50.726 --> 00:19:53.904
It was all paid for by our parents.

273
00:19:53.904 --> 00:19:59.466
Well, my dad because he worked, but my mother took care of the finances.

274
00:19:59.466 --> 00:20:01.849
So it was a great opportunity.

275
00:20:01.849 --> 00:20:06.219
A lot of kids didn't have that opportunity, but we did.

276
00:20:06.219 --> 00:20:09.000
<v DRAGOON>What did you study at McNeese?

277
00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:11.507
<v JONES>Office administration and I worked there.

278
00:20:11.507 --> 00:20:21.537
Then I signed up with [Kelly Girl?], so I got an opportunity to work at a couple of the different industries in the area.

279
00:20:21.537 --> 00:20:29.060
Then eventually I got hired at . . . It was Continental Oil Company at the time when I got hired.

280
00:20:29.060 --> 00:20:29.478
I

281
00:20:29.478 --> 00:20:31.567
<c.p>worked there until I retired.

282
00:20:31.567 --> 00:20:34.075
<v DRAGOON>What were you doing for them?

283
00:20:34.075 --> 00:20:43.269
<v JONES>By the end of my career I was doing . . . I worked in a property tax real estate right-of-way for pipelines and claims.

284
00:20:43.269 --> 00:20:50.791
If there was anything like an incident from the plant then I got called out for emergency response.

285
00:20:50.791 --> 00:20:55.806
So it was an interesting job once I got into that department.

286
00:20:55.806 --> 00:20:59.149
<v DRAGOON>What kind of incidents were you dealing with?

287
00:20:59.149 --> 00:21:01.239
<c.p>[portion removed per interviewee’s request]

288
00:21:01.239 --> 00:21:05.000
<v DRAGOON>Were these all plant workers that you were . . .?

289
00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:13.982
<v JONES>No sometimes they would . . . It was different individuals that would call in sometimes and say something about they . . . or either a lot of times they'd say,

290
00:21:13.982 --> 00:21:19.164
"Well I heard somebody say that this happened and so I was in the area."

291
00:21:19.164 --> 00:21:27.109
So that's why I would take the information from them and see if someone else would be able to assist them with it.

292
00:21:27.109 --> 00:21:27.455
<v DRAGOON>Wow.

293
00:21:27.455 --> 00:21:27.800
<v JONES>Yeah.

294
00:21:27.800 --> 00:21:31.255
Now that's the part I probably need to have restricted.

295
00:21:31.255 --> 00:21:32.291
Where I can't

296
00:21:32.291 --> 00:21:36.091
<c.p>because . . . In case it should turn into some type of lawsuit.

297
00:21:36.091 --> 00:21:36.436
<v DRAGOON>Yeah.

298
00:21:36.436 --> 00:21:37.473
<v JONES>From the investigation.

299
00:21:37.473 --> 00:21:40.582
So I would like to have that part restricted.

300
00:21:40.582 --> 00:21:40.927
<v DRAGOON>Sure.

301
00:21:40.927 --> 00:21:41.273
<v JONES>Okay.

302
00:21:41.273 --> 00:21:42.309
<v DRAGOON>That's no problem.

303
00:21:42.309 --> 00:21:43.000
<v JONES>Alright.

304
00:21:43.000 --> 00:21:49.842
<v DRAGOON>Do you want to make a note and then we'll talk to Jen about it?

305
00:21:49.842 --> 00:21:50.298
<v JONES>Okay.

306
00:21:50.298 --> 00:21:57.596
<v DRAGOON>I have so many questions about that. [laughs] But we can come back to it later.

307
00:21:57.596 --> 00:21:58.053
<v JONES>Okay.

308
00:21:58.053 --> 00:22:01.702
<v DRAGOON>So what kind of . . . Is that a train?

309
00:22:01.702 --> 00:22:04.895
<v JONES>Yes it is and it's real close.

310
00:22:04.895 --> 00:22:05.351
<v DRAGOON>Yeah.

311
00:22:05.351 --> 00:22:07.175
It just won't stop.

312
00:22:07.175 --> 00:22:09.000
<v JONES>It's real close.

313
00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:13.608
<v DRAGOON>What kind of big memories stand out from your childhood in Mossville?

314
00:22:13.608 --> 00:22:27.431
<v JONES>Let's see, well I can remember my mother and some of the ladies in the neighborhood getting us all together and taking us to walk down to the Westlake areas so that we could go crabbing.

315
00:22:27.431 --> 00:22:29.735
That was always a big thing.

316
00:22:29.735 --> 00:22:32.423
Everybody liked to fish and to crab.

317
00:22:32.423 --> 00:22:36.646
So that was one of the big things for the children.

318
00:22:36.646 --> 00:22:40.486
They’d come back and get together and boil the crabs.

319
00:22:40.486 --> 00:22:44.710
Then all of us would sit down and eat crabs, so.

320
00:22:44.710 --> 00:22:48.166
Now my mother didn't fish, but my grandmother did.

321
00:22:48.166 --> 00:22:53.925
So I would go fishing with her and her sisters when they would get together.

322
00:22:53.925 --> 00:23:05.445
But just from the community . . . Also we'd get together and our parents would walk us to the recreation center and let us spend some time there on the playground equipment.

323
00:23:05.445 --> 00:23:07.749
Also going to the swimming pool.

324
00:23:07.749 --> 00:23:12.356
There was a swimming pool and we would learn to swim there.

325
00:23:12.356 --> 00:23:13.124
Took lessons.

326
00:23:13.124 --> 00:23:27.331
Then eventually as we got to be teenagers well then we could walk by ourselves when they felt we were responsible enough to walk down from where we lived to the swimming pool we could do that.

327
00:23:27.331 --> 00:23:30.403
<c.p>So those were some of the enjoyable times.

328
00:23:30.403 --> 00:23:31.939
Also the record hops.

329
00:23:31.939 --> 00:23:39.619
Like I said, we'd have one of the local disc jockeys, he'd come to Mossville at night once a week.

330
00:23:39.619 --> 00:23:48.834
He'd have what they call teen town and all the teenagers would show up and you'd pay to get in, listen to the music.

331
00:23:48.834 --> 00:23:54.210
You'd get to dance and once it was over with everybody would go home.

332
00:23:54.210 --> 00:23:58.818
But that was a great opportunity for us all to get together.

333
00:23:58.818 --> 00:24:04.193
Plus we all . . . A lot of the young ladies, we all liked to sew.

334
00:24:04.193 --> 00:24:12.257
So we learned to sew and we were always buying patterns and switching patterns out with one another to make clothes.

335
00:24:12.257 --> 00:24:19.936
Even though our families would buy us clothes, we just liked the idea of having that opportunity of getting together,

336
00:24:19.936 --> 00:24:28.000
going to the store, and finding material, and coming back home and making pantsuits back then when they became popular.

337
00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:29.102
<v DRAGOON>That sounds amazing.

338
00:24:29.102 --> 00:24:32.773
I would love to see a picture of a pantsuit.

339
00:24:32.773 --> 00:24:35.344
<v JONES>Oh I have plenty of those [laughs].

340
00:24:35.344 --> 00:24:38.648
Oh, yeah and taking pictures, that was another thing.

341
00:24:38.648 --> 00:24:44.523
I would always have a camera so I was always taking pictures when we'd get together.

342
00:24:44.523 --> 00:24:48.195
And we’d . . . That was another thing we’d do on weekends.

343
00:24:48.195 --> 00:24:50.766
I had a little portable record player.

344
00:24:50.766 --> 00:24:54.070
It was battery operated and it played forty-five records.

345
00:24:54.070 --> 00:24:57.742
So we'd walk through the neighborhood on Sundays after church.

346
00:24:57.742 --> 00:25:04.719
Everybody would put . . . we'd put our short sets on and just walk through the neighborhood just listening to music.

347
00:25:04.719 --> 00:25:08.023
Not going anywhere in particular, but just walking around.

348
00:25:08.023 --> 00:25:11.695
So that was a great opportunity to get exercise in.

349
00:25:11.695 --> 00:25:15.000
<v DRAGOON>What kind of music were you listening to?

350
00:25:15.000 --> 00:25:21.429
<v JONES>By that time we were starting to listen to Aretha Franklin, James Brown, Otis Redding . . . Some Otis Redding.

351
00:25:21.429 --> 00:25:25.000
I'm trying to think, who else was popular back then?

352
00:25:25.000 --> 00:25:30.714
The Supremes, the Temptations, so those were some of the groups we listened to a lot.

353
00:25:30.714 --> 00:25:36.786
Everybody would buy music and if you had the latest music we'd say, "Hey we're going walking."

354
00:25:36.786 --> 00:25:40.357
We'd call them up and say, "Hey we're going walking.

355
00:25:40.357 --> 00:25:41.429
Bring your records."

356
00:25:41.429 --> 00:25:44.286
So they'd bring their records and come out.

357
00:25:44.286 --> 00:25:52.500
And we'd . . . Somebody’d walk around with a little hand loaded records [player] and we'd just switch them out as we walked and talked.

358
00:25:52.500 --> 00:25:57.143
We'd stop and we'd go to somebody's house and get a blanket out.

359
00:25:57.143 --> 00:26:01.429
Lay under the shade trees and listen to music and just talk.

360
00:26:01.429 --> 00:26:07.857
A lot of the guys in the neighborhood, or some of the guys we went to school with,

361
00:26:07.857 --> 00:26:12.143
they would come into our neighborhood and stop and talk with us.

362
00:26:12.143 --> 00:26:14.643
So it was just a great opportunity.

363
00:26:14.643 --> 00:26:22.500
We didn't have ice cream stands like they did in Westlake that you could go to, but it didn't matter to us.

364
00:26:22.500 --> 00:26:27.143
It was just an opportunity of everybody getting together and just being there.

365
00:26:27.143 --> 00:26:28.929
Sharing stories with each other.

366
00:26:28.929 --> 00:26:33.929
Talking about who was the cute guys in school and that kind of stuff.

367
00:26:33.929 --> 00:26:40.000
<v DRAGOON>Did you . . . Have you kept in touch with some old friends that you grew up with?

368
00:26:40.000 --> 00:26:40.896
<v JONES>Oh yes.

369
00:26:40.896 --> 00:26:42.687
<v DRAGOON>A lot of them?

370
00:26:42.687 --> 00:26:43.134
<v JONES>Yes.

371
00:26:43.134 --> 00:26:45.821
Yeah I'm one of those people.

372
00:26:45.821 --> 00:26:49.851
I usually go to almost everything I would get

373
00:26:49.851 --> 00:26:50.746
<c.p>invited to.

374
00:26:50.746 --> 00:26:56.119
If it was weddings, or their children’s graduations, I go to those.

375
00:26:56.119 --> 00:26:57.015
Baby showers.

376
00:26:57.015 --> 00:26:57.463
Funerals.

377
00:26:57.463 --> 00:27:02.388
I usually try to . . . If I wasn't out of town working,

378
00:27:02.388 --> 00:27:10.448
well then I would always try to make it to some of those functions that were going on.

379
00:27:10.448 --> 00:27:13.582
Did back then and I still do.

380
00:27:13.582 --> 00:27:18.060
I'm just a little older and a little slower now.

381
00:27:18.060 --> 00:27:22.090
<v DRAGOON>And where did you go to church growing up?

382
00:27:22.090 --> 00:27:29.701
<v JONES>Over in the Willow Springs area between Sulphur and Dequincy where my grandparents lived. [knock on door]

383
00:27:29.701 --> 00:27:31.940
<v DRAGOON>Let’s pause [break in tape].

384
00:27:31.940 --> 00:27:35.522
Alright and we're back after a brief break.

385
00:27:35.522 --> 00:27:40.000
Sorry could you repeat where you went to church?

386
00:27:40.000 --> 00:27:40.407
<v JONES>Sure.

387
00:27:40.407 --> 00:27:46.512
I belonged to the Willow Spring Baptist Church, and it's located between Sulphur and Dequincy.

388
00:27:46.512 --> 00:27:55.872
That's the church that my grandfather belonged to and it was just located down the street from where he and my grandmother lived.

389
00:27:55.872 --> 00:27:59.535
We had a lot of family that belonged there.

390
00:27:59.535 --> 00:28:04.826
My grandfather's siblings and my grandmother's siblings had also belonged to the church.

391
00:28:04.826 --> 00:28:07.674
So I call it my family church.

392
00:28:07.674 --> 00:28:11.337
<v DRAGOON>What did a typical Sunday look like growing up?

393
00:28:11.337 --> 00:28:22.326
<v JONES>Typical Sunday . . . It was real quiet the early part of the morning if we were at home because a lot of the neighbors had gone to church.

394
00:28:22.326 --> 00:28:25.174
They had taken their children to Sunday

395
00:28:25.174 --> 00:28:28.430
<c.p>school and that was early in the mornings.

396
00:28:28.430 --> 00:28:44.709
Then after what was church service held it was about . . . I guess about three everybody would start . . . coming back out because they'd come back home from church and they'd eat and change clothes and they were just ready to visit.

397
00:28:44.709 --> 00:28:45.930
Just still relaxing.

398
00:28:45.930 --> 00:28:52.035
Everybody would just kind of walk around in the neighborhood talking about the upcoming week.

399
00:28:52.035 --> 00:28:59.360
The parents would be out and they would always . . . we called it “holler” at each other back then.

400
00:28:59.360 --> 00:29:01.802
But it was pretty quiet still.

401
00:29:01.802 --> 00:29:05.872
You would see . . . For some of the neighbors, excuse me,

402
00:29:05.872 --> 00:29:17.674
they didn't have a car so you would see them walking to catch a ride with somebody that was going to work in the area where they were working,

403
00:29:17.674 --> 00:29:21.337
or asking to be dropped off on their way.

404
00:29:21.337 --> 00:29:25.000
<v DRAGOON>Was your family very active in the church?

405
00:29:25.000 --> 00:29:26.772
<v JONES>No. No they weren't.

406
00:29:26.772 --> 00:29:31.202
I was the only one that would go to church.

407
00:29:31.202 --> 00:29:39.619
Of course like I said because my dad worked out of town a lot . . . And he drove cross country.

408
00:29:39.619 --> 00:29:46.264
And then my mother she would go on some of the road trips with him.

409
00:29:46.264 --> 00:29:51.137
Back in those days you could take your spouse with you.

410
00:29:51.137 --> 00:30:01.769
So they got to go, but I was perfectly content to stay with my grandparents and my grandfather and I would go to church.

411
00:30:01.769 --> 00:30:11.515
My grandmother had belonged to a different church than my grandfather and so I just ended up going to church with him.

412
00:30:11.515 --> 00:30:15.502
I stayed a member there for years and years.

413
00:30:15.502 --> 00:30:22.147
I left and then I went back and I'm still a member there now, so.

414
00:30:22.147 --> 00:30:26.134
One of my relatives from Mossville is my pastor.

415
00:30:26.134 --> 00:30:31.007
<v DRAGOON>Let’s see . . . What was the role of women in the church?

416
00:30:31.007 --> 00:30:38.094
<v JONES>They were deaconess in the church, the women were, and they were leaders of the choir.

417
00:30:38.094 --> 00:30:45.625
We have male musicians, piano players, but a lot of the leaders of the choirs were women.

418
00:30:45.625 --> 00:30:50.498
We had women on the usher board and Sunday school teachers.

419
00:30:50.498 --> 00:30:52.713
Let’s see . . . Over the cafeteria.

420
00:30:52.713 --> 00:30:56.257
So they were real active in the church.

421
00:30:56.257 --> 00:31:11.319
Always making sure that when the holidays rolled around there was some type of program for the young children to participate in because they felt that the youth were the future of the church.

422
00:31:11.319 --> 00:31:19.293
And that they could keep them involved in different activities, well then they would want to continue coming,

423
00:31:19.293 --> 00:31:24.609
the young children, and then bring in more young people with them.

424
00:31:24.609 --> 00:31:27.710
Some of their friends, or maybe neighbors.

425
00:31:27.710 --> 00:31:32.140
So we did a lot of visiting other churches, also.

426
00:31:32.140 --> 00:31:33.026
Other faiths.

427
00:31:33.026 --> 00:31:37.899
So it was . . . But they were very active in the church.

428
00:31:37.899 --> 00:31:41.000
<v DRAGOON>Were your parents very religious people?

429
00:31:41.000 --> 00:31:47.485
<v JONES>My mother would not attend church every Sunday or anything, but she read the Bible every day.

430
00:31:47.485 --> 00:31:50.537
She had completed reading the Bible several times.

431
00:31:50.537 --> 00:31:53.970
I mean she knew it from cover to cover.

432
00:31:53.970 --> 00:31:55.878
She just wasn't always there.

433
00:31:55.878 --> 00:31:59.311
And then later she joined and she was active.

434
00:31:59.311 --> 00:32:02.744
An active member after she did join the church.

435
00:32:02.744 --> 00:32:06.941
Then she got sick and she just broke away from going.

436
00:32:06.941 --> 00:32:10.374
She wasn’t . . . Her health didn't allow her to go.

437
00:32:10.374 --> 00:32:17.622
And so . . . But I have had continued to go and keep her updated as to the preaching and everything.

438
00:32:17.622 --> 00:32:23.726
Sometimes we tape the services and bring them back and let her listen to the services.

439
00:32:23.726 --> 00:32:26.015
But she used to like revivals.

440
00:32:26.015 --> 00:32:29.067
That was another thing we had years ago.

441
00:32:29.067 --> 00:32:34.026
We used to have a lot of tent revivals coming into the community.

442
00:32:34.026 --> 00:32:41.274
Preachers from other areas would come and they'd find a vacant lot and they'd put up a big tent.

443
00:32:41.274 --> 00:32:46.233
There would be chairs and they would have their choir members with them.

444
00:32:46.233 --> 00:32:49.667
There was singing and praying that would go on.

445
00:32:49.667 --> 00:32:51.193
A lot of the

446
00:32:51.193 --> 00:32:55.389
<c.p>neighbors would attend even if that was not of their faith.

447
00:32:55.389 --> 00:33:01.111
You would hear it and it was kind of like the music caught your attention.

448
00:33:01.111 --> 00:33:03.400
You would head in that direction.

449
00:33:03.400 --> 00:33:07.978
Your feet would go to tapping because you could hear the music.

450
00:33:07.978 --> 00:33:12.174
And so of course the children we all wanted to go.

451
00:33:12.174 --> 00:33:16.370
It gave us an opportunity of course to stay up late.

452
00:33:16.370 --> 00:33:21.711
But we would still go and listen and get the word at that time.

453
00:33:21.711 --> 00:33:24.000
<v DRAGOON>Was that happening in Mossville?

454
00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:27.874
<v JONES>In the area that we lived in.

455
00:33:27.874 --> 00:33:30.641
The Lincoln Heights subdivision, yeah.

456
00:33:30.641 --> 00:33:33.961
<v DRAGOON>Who were your childhood role models?

457
00:33:33.961 --> 00:33:38.388
<v JONES>You know, I never ever thought of that.

458
00:33:38.388 --> 00:33:45.583
I don't know I guess because mostly everybody was close to my age.

459
00:33:45.583 --> 00:33:52.223
If they were older than us, they didn't hang around with us.

460
00:33:52.223 --> 00:33:54.990
They had their own agendas.

461
00:33:54.990 --> 00:33:57.757
Their own groups of friends.

462
00:33:57.757 --> 00:34:06.612
But basically all of us that were the same age is who we would be with.

463
00:34:06.612 --> 00:34:12.699
<v DRAGOON>Sorry I'm trying to decide which one to ask you next.

464
00:34:12.699 --> 00:34:21.000
What kinds of values do you think your parents instilled in you growing up?

465
00:34:21.000 --> 00:34:22.964
<v JONES>To be kind to everybody.

466
00:34:22.964 --> 00:34:24.143
Everybody was somebody.

467
00:34:24.143 --> 00:34:26.107
There was not a nobody.

468
00:34:26.107 --> 00:34:28.857
Everybody had to be treated the same.

469
00:34:28.857 --> 00:34:39.071
I don't care if it was a person that was hitchhiking a ride . . . Back in the sixties and early seventies you could give hitchhikers a ride,

470
00:34:39.071 --> 00:34:40.250
but they were

471
00:34:40.250 --> 00:34:41.429
<c.p>treated with respect.

472
00:34:41.429 --> 00:34:47.714
It was a, “Yes sir,” to them just like if it was one of our neighbors.

473
00:34:47.714 --> 00:34:51.250
They weren't treated any different than anybody else was.

474
00:34:51.250 --> 00:34:54.393
I was always taught to respect my elders.

475
00:34:54.393 --> 00:34:56.750
To always help whenever I could.

476
00:34:56.750 --> 00:35:00.679
Those were just some things that always stuck to me.

477
00:35:00.679 --> 00:35:06.179
I was never better than anybody, but I was never less than anybody either.

478
00:35:06.179 --> 00:35:09.321
We were all on the same playing field.

479
00:35:09.321 --> 00:35:21.107
Because I was always taught whatever you do to somebody it's going to come back to you and it's not going to feel good when it comes back to you.

480
00:35:21.107 --> 00:35:27.000
It wasn't called karma back then, but it was just taught to be kind.

481
00:35:27.000 --> 00:35:33.128
Another thing I remember hearing a lot not just from my mother but from my grandparents,

482
00:35:33.128 --> 00:35:37.340
my grandmother's favorite thing was, "It's nothing like a bought lesson."

483
00:35:37.340 --> 00:35:44.617
She said, "You can tell a child that . . ." She had a wood heater in her house and she said,

484
00:35:44.617 --> 00:35:52.660
"I could tell you that that heater is hot if you touch it with your hand you're going to be burned."

485
00:35:52.660 --> 00:36:00.319
She said, "But if you don't listen to me you're going to go and you're going to do it anyway."

486
00:36:00.319 --> 00:36:06.830
And she said, "Once your hand is burned you're going to know what I said was true."

487
00:36:06.830 --> 00:36:10.277
So she said, "It's nothing like a bought lesson."

488
00:36:10.277 --> 00:36:18.319
Sometimes you have to let a person experience things for themselves before they understand that you're telling them the right thing,

489
00:36:18.319 --> 00:36:23.681
not something to hurt them, but something to guide them in the right direction.

490
00:36:23.681 --> 00:36:27.128
And my mother was always a very kind person.

491
00:36:27.128 --> 00:36:33.638
She always did what she could to help somebody and it was always one of those things,

492
00:36:33.638 --> 00:36:38.617
"Do what you can to help a person, nothing to hurt a person."

493
00:36:38.617 --> 00:36:44.745
Because if you don't want to be hurt then you should not hurt somebody on purpose.

494
00:36:44.745 --> 00:36:52.787
If you do something and if it's wrong you need to go back and tell them that you're sorry for it.

495
00:36:52.787 --> 00:36:57.000
And don't say you're sorry if you're not really sorry.

496
00:36:57.000 --> 00:37:01.048
<v DRAGOON>How were decisions made in your family between your parents

497
00:37:01.048 --> 00:37:01.857
<c.p>and you?

498
00:37:01.857 --> 00:37:03.476
<v JONES>Basically by my mother.

499
00:37:03.476 --> 00:37:05.905
She was always the forceful one.

500
00:37:05.905 --> 00:37:11.976
My dad . . . He was kind of laid back and sometimes he would say something different.

501
00:37:11.976 --> 00:37:20.476
If she said no then I could catch him by himself maybe and talk to him and maybe he'd say yes.

502
00:37:20.476 --> 00:37:25.738
But usually he'd say, "You have to go by what your mom said."

503
00:37:25.738 --> 00:37:33.429
And that was her rule because he wasn't there a lot and she didn't want either one of us,

504
00:37:33.429 --> 00:37:43.952
or her nieces and nephews that would come by, she didn't want them going to him asking for something that she had told them no on.

505
00:37:43.952 --> 00:37:48.000
<v DRAGOON>Do you think she was a particularly strict parent?

506
00:37:48.000 --> 00:37:48.674
<v JONES>Yes.

507
00:37:48.674 --> 00:37:50.023
Oh, yes.

508
00:37:50.023 --> 00:37:59.465
If I did something that was wrong I would get a spanking for it.

509
00:37:59.465 --> 00:38:05.535
That was not anything that I could get around.

510
00:38:05.535 --> 00:38:14.302
<v DRAGOON>I want to ask you . . . So you mentioned you got married in seventy . . .

511
00:38:14.302 --> 00:38:14.977
<v JONES>Six.

512
00:38:14.977 --> 00:38:15.651
<v DRAGOON>'76?

513
00:38:15.651 --> 00:38:17.000
<v JONES>Yes.

514
00:38:17.000 --> 00:38:20.053
<v DRAGOON>Did you meet . . . What's your husband's name?

515
00:38:20.053 --> 00:38:20.489
<v JONES>Hayward.

516
00:38:20.489 --> 00:38:20.926
<v DRAGOON>Hayward?

517
00:38:20.926 --> 00:38:21.362
<v JONES>[agrees]

518
00:38:21.362 --> 00:38:24.415
<v DRAGOON>Did you meet him in high school?

519
00:38:24.415 --> 00:38:27.904
<v JONES>When his family moved to the Mossville community.

520
00:38:27.904 --> 00:38:28.777
<v DRAGOON>Oh, right.

521
00:38:28.777 --> 00:38:29.213
<v JONES>Yeah.

522
00:38:29.213 --> 00:38:34.883
They had lived in Houston [Texas] and they moved to Mossville in 1969.

523
00:38:34.883 --> 00:38:40.989
One of . . . They were relatives to another family that lived next door to us.

524
00:38:40.989 --> 00:38:44.479
So that's how I got to meet them.

525
00:38:44.479 --> 00:38:54.074
They . . . my husband had . . . He's from a family of eight children so Mossville was a small town for them coming from Houston.

526
00:38:54.074 --> 00:38:58.000
<v DRAGOON>How old were you when you got married?

527
00:38:58.000 --> 00:38:58.403
<v JONES>Twenty-one.

528
00:38:58.403 --> 00:39:00.820
I made twenty-two the next month.

529
00:39:00.820 --> 00:39:04.043
<v DRAGOON>And did he go to McNeese as well?

530
00:39:04.043 --> 00:39:05.252
<v JONES>No, he didn't.

531
00:39:05.252 --> 00:39:14.518
He worked for a while over in Orange [Texas] at the shipyard and then he got a job over in the Westlake area.

532
00:39:14.518 --> 00:39:16.532
<v DRAGOON>And do you have children?

533
00:39:16.532 --> 00:39:17.741
<v JONES>Yes I do.

534
00:39:17.741 --> 00:39:19.353
I have two children.

535
00:39:19.353 --> 00:39:20.561
They're both grown.

536
00:39:20.561 --> 00:39:21.770
Neither one's married.

537
00:39:21.770 --> 00:39:23.784
I don't have any grandchildren.

538
00:39:23.784 --> 00:39:27.007
I have a grand-puppy as everybody calls him.

539
00:39:27.007 --> 00:39:30.633
But yeah they both live here in Lake Charles.

540
00:39:30.633 --> 00:39:33.453
<v DRAGOON>So they grew up in Lake Charles?

541
00:39:33.453 --> 00:39:34.662
<v JONES>Yes they did.

542
00:39:34.662 --> 00:39:39.496
Now for a while . . . Back in 1991 we moved back to Mossville.

543
00:39:39.496 --> 00:39:49.568
We had sold our house and when we put it on the market then we moved back to Mossville and we lived with my mother.

544
00:39:49.568 --> 00:39:54.000
<v DRAGOON>How long were you living with her in the nineties?

545
00:39:54.000 --> 00:39:58.289
<v JONES>Around I guess about nine months or maybe a little longer.

546
00:39:58.289 --> 00:40:01.408
<v DRAGOON>Was it strange for you to move back . . .

547
00:40:01.408 --> 00:40:01.798
<v JONES>No.

548
00:40:01.798 --> 00:40:03.358
<v DRAGOON>To your childhood area?

549
00:40:03.358 --> 00:40:04.917
<v JONES>No. Not at all.

550
00:40:04.917 --> 00:40:12.716
No because I had always . . . Even though I had married and moved to Lake Charles we attended church in Sulphur,

551
00:40:12.716 --> 00:40:16.225
but my in-laws lived two blocks from my mother.

552
00:40:16.225 --> 00:40:21.683
So we were always back in the community and we kept the same friends.

553
00:40:21.683 --> 00:40:24.803
We'd see them at different functions and activities.

554
00:40:24.803 --> 00:40:27.142
So it was . . . Nothing had changed.

555
00:40:27.142 --> 00:40:33.381
It was just like back in the old days when I moved back with my mother.

556
00:40:33.381 --> 00:40:40.399
I was grown and had children, but her rules were still her rules because it was her house.

557
00:40:40.399 --> 00:40:45.078
So she was glad to have us because she had company then.

558
00:40:45.078 --> 00:40:50.537
I guess my sister had married and had moved to Houston by that time.

559
00:40:50.537 --> 00:40:52.876
And so . . . But we enjoyed it.

560
00:40:52.876 --> 00:41:00.674
My children enjoyed it because they could walk down to my mother-in-law’s and visit with other relatives that were there.

561
00:41:00.674 --> 00:41:06.133
And like I said, they knew the other children that were in the neighborhood.

562
00:41:06.133 --> 00:41:11.592
My son went to school in Westlake while we were living on that side.

563
00:41:11.592 --> 00:41:14.711
<v DRAGOON>What did you notice was different about Mossville?

564
00:41:14.711 --> 00:41:17.050
Like moving back in the nineties.

565
00:41:17.050 --> 00:41:19.000
Did it feel different?

566
00:41:19.000 --> 00:41:20.551
<v JONES>To me it didn't.

567
00:41:20.551 --> 00:41:20.939
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

568
00:41:20.939 --> 00:41:21.327
<v JONES>Yeah.

569
00:41:21.327 --> 00:41:24.042
To me it was still the same.

570
00:41:24.042 --> 00:41:29.084
Just everybody that I had grown up with basically of course was older,

571
00:41:29.084 --> 00:41:39.944
but I still . . . I can remember sitting outside at my mother's and we used to play ball in the evenings after school where Entergy had their high line.

572
00:41:39.944 --> 00:41:41.495
It was a cleared

573
00:41:41.495 --> 00:41:48.477
<c.p>area so we’d get out there and we’d divide up, make two ball teams, and we’d play ball.

574
00:41:48.477 --> 00:41:55.458
We'd laugh and talk about how we played ball and who had the best team and such, so.

575
00:41:55.458 --> 00:42:05.542
And it was fun because I told them, "I always knew that every time there was a ball game I was going to get to play."

576
00:42:05.542 --> 00:42:15.626
And that was only because I had the ball, the bat, and the glove. [laughs] So I knew that I was going to get to play.

577
00:42:15.626 --> 00:42:17.953
So we always laughed about that.

578
00:42:17.953 --> 00:42:20.280
But everybody still was the same.

579
00:42:20.280 --> 00:42:25.710
We were still in . . . In the early seventies some of our classmates got married.

580
00:42:25.710 --> 00:42:33.079
We were in their weddings and then when we got married some of the classmates were in our weddings.

581
00:42:33.079 --> 00:42:39.285
Like I said, we did . . . We'd go to the showers, bridal, baby showers for each other.

582
00:42:39.285 --> 00:42:42.000
<v DRAGOON>What were weddings like in Mossville?

583
00:42:42.000 --> 00:42:45.708
<v JONES>Our weddings were nice because the brides always got together.

584
00:42:45.708 --> 00:42:51.640
Picked out girls from the neighborhood and we made our gowns to be in the weddings.

585
00:42:51.640 --> 00:42:55.348
We'd get together go select the pattern and the material.

586
00:42:55.348 --> 00:42:57.202
Get together, make the gowns.

587
00:42:57.202 --> 00:43:04.989
But then the family, the bride's family, always had a big dinner at their house when the wedding was over with.

588
00:43:04.989 --> 00:43:12.034
So you didn't go to places like they do now where you're finding a venue to have a reception.

589
00:43:12.034 --> 00:43:14.258
That was all done at home.

590
00:43:14.258 --> 00:43:23.899
You had cake and punch at the church, but the families always served food for their out of town family members at home before the wedding.

591
00:43:23.899 --> 00:43:28.719
Then when the wedding was over with, everybody that went to the wedding,

592
00:43:28.719 --> 00:43:34.281
plus the out of town family members, were there and you got to eat again.

593
00:43:34.281 --> 00:43:43.921
It was just tons of food that was cooked and there was music and everybody standing around laughing and talking and watching the little kids dance.

594
00:43:43.921 --> 00:43:49.483
Just way into the night because all the weddings usually were early in the evening.

595
00:43:49.483 --> 00:43:53.191
So if it was summer and it wasn't too hot

596
00:43:53.191 --> 00:43:55.787
<c.p>people stayed a little bit longer outside.

597
00:43:55.787 --> 00:44:00.607
They weren't all worried about going in to be under the air conditioner.

598
00:44:00.607 --> 00:44:07.281
But then for years when weddings took place their houses didn't have air conditioning, so everybody stayed outside.

599
00:44:07.281 --> 00:44:10.247
You were under the shade trees or something.

600
00:44:10.247 --> 00:44:11.360
Big window fans.

601
00:44:11.360 --> 00:44:16.180
I remember we had those in the house too, to keep it cool.

602
00:44:16.180 --> 00:44:21.000
<v DRAGOON>So did you have a big wedding reception at your parents' house?

603
00:44:21.000 --> 00:44:22.087
<v JONES>Yes I did.

604
00:44:22.087 --> 00:44:24.987
Yeah we had the reception at our house.

605
00:44:24.987 --> 00:44:27.886
We served cake and punch at the church.

606
00:44:27.886 --> 00:44:31.148
Then we went home . . . Went to my parents' house.

607
00:44:31.148 --> 00:44:38.396
And my in laws had also cooked because they had family from out of town, but it was the same.

608
00:44:38.396 --> 00:44:47.094
We had barbeque and all the side dishes to go with it and served everybody at home and sent food home with people, so.

609
00:44:47.094 --> 00:44:51.805
It was just one of those type of things that everybody just enjoyed.

610
00:44:51.805 --> 00:44:57.966
Some families would use the recreation center in Mossville by the Rigmaiden Center and have receptions there.

611
00:44:57.966 --> 00:45:06.302
But I didn't have a big family, so we weren't going to have a lot of people coming in from out of town.

612
00:45:06.302 --> 00:45:09.926
It was just basically the neighbors and my dad's coworkers.

613
00:45:09.926 --> 00:45:14.275
<v DRAGOON>What was one of the greatest joys of your childhood in Mossville?

614
00:45:14.275 --> 00:45:16.450
<v JONES>I guess going to . . . Starting school.

615
00:45:16.450 --> 00:45:17.537
Back to school.

616
00:45:17.537 --> 00:45:24.785
Getting to see everybody because when we returned back to school . . . That was one of the things in middle school.

617
00:45:24.785 --> 00:45:28.409
They would always let you talk about your summer vacations.

618
00:45:28.409 --> 00:45:37.832
What you did, if you went away for a trip, and just getting to hear about what everybody else had done because it sounded so exciting.

619
00:45:37.832 --> 00:45:44.718
If you heard somebody say they got to go to Galveston [Texas] to a beach and you listened to

620
00:45:44.718 --> 00:45:50.517
<c.p>them talk about the water and swimming, or if someone happened to go out of state.

621
00:45:50.517 --> 00:45:53.779
Those type of things was always fun to me.

622
00:45:53.779 --> 00:46:01.752
And like I said, for a long time I was by myself so it didn't take too much to get me happy.

623
00:46:01.752 --> 00:46:04.289
Just to be with the other children.

624
00:46:04.289 --> 00:46:05.738
That was good enough.

625
00:46:05.738 --> 00:46:09.000
<v DRAGOON>And what did you do during the summer?

626
00:46:09.000 --> 00:46:13.370
<v JONES>Basically we always had a lot of children at our house.

627
00:46:13.370 --> 00:46:19.329
Of course everybody liked coming to my house because I had a swing set also.

628
00:46:19.329 --> 00:46:20.521
So they . . . [laughs].

629
00:46:20.521 --> 00:46:28.466
And parents when they would come over my mother drank coffee, so she always had a pot of coffee going.

630
00:46:28.466 --> 00:46:49.521
And so a lot of the other mothers would come and they'd sit under the . . . We had a shade tree in the back and one in the front so the kids could get on the swing set and play or kick ball and the parents would sit and drink their coffee and talk.

631
00:46:49.521 --> 00:46:57.863
We’d just have a good time until it was time for nap time and then everybody started leaving and going home.

632
00:46:57.863 --> 00:47:02.233
<v DRAGOON>What kinds of big social events did you have growing up?

633
00:47:02.233 --> 00:47:03.027
<v JONES>Social events.

634
00:47:03.027 --> 00:47:04.616
I'm trying to think . . .

635
00:47:04.616 --> 00:47:06.205
<v DRAGOON>Like big get togethers.

636
00:47:06.205 --> 00:47:07.000
Barbeques.

637
00:47:07.000 --> 00:47:13.444
<v JONES>We didn't really have any big get togethers because my mother had a small family.

638
00:47:13.444 --> 00:47:21.178
She had one sister and one brother and I can remember every Sunday we were at my grandmother's.

639
00:47:21.178 --> 00:47:25.904
So when people started talking about family reunions and getting together

640
00:47:25.904 --> 00:47:34.496
<c.p>and like going to their grandparents' and all the other relatives coming and I was like, "That's not nothing new."

641
00:47:34.496 --> 00:47:47.385
I thought it was a little boring until I got to understand that they did a little bit more than all get together and sit around and talk and eat.

642
00:47:47.385 --> 00:47:50.822
But that was about it, like I said.

643
00:47:50.822 --> 00:47:54.689
Then I didn't know anybody from my dad's side.

644
00:47:54.689 --> 00:48:00.704
So it was just basically over to my mom's mom and that was it.

645
00:48:00.704 --> 00:48:05.000
<v DRAGOON>Did anyone in your family serve in the military?

646
00:48:05.000 --> 00:48:05.458
<v JONES>No.

647
00:48:05.458 --> 00:48:07.289
<v DRAGOON>Changing a little bit . . .

648
00:48:07.289 --> 00:48:07.746
<v JONES>Okay.

649
00:48:07.746 --> 00:48:14.155
<v DRAGOON>Where I'm going with this. [laughs] Did you learn to cook from your mother?

650
00:48:14.155 --> 00:48:19.648
<v JONES>Yes I did, but it was after I was grown and married.

651
00:48:19.648 --> 00:48:21.937
She did all the cooking.

652
00:48:21.937 --> 00:48:29.718
She didn't want me around the stove because I might get burned, or set something on fire.

653
00:48:29.718 --> 00:48:32.007
That was what she did.

654
00:48:32.007 --> 00:48:41.620
She just would do all the cooking so I didn't have to worry about . . . I didn't have to do any housework.

655
00:48:41.620 --> 00:48:45.282
I didn't have to do any cooking, so.

656
00:48:45.282 --> 00:48:50.775
But it was one of the funny stories that I tell everybody.

657
00:48:50.775 --> 00:49:01.303
I had some relatives that lived in Lake Charles, so when I'd go visit them they'd let me do all their work, so.

658
00:49:01.303 --> 00:49:04.965
But I just didn't have to do any.

659
00:49:04.965 --> 00:49:10.000
<v DRAGOON>What kinds of dishes bring back memories from your childhood?

660
00:49:10.000 --> 00:49:17.107
<v JONES>Some of the dishes with the wheat [design] in the center on the little dishes that you’d get.

661
00:49:17.107 --> 00:49:25.004
And you all are young so you don't remember it, but the ones you get with the box of oatmeal.

662
00:49:25.004 --> 00:49:27.373
They were either pink or green.

663
00:49:27.373 --> 00:49:34.876
You'd have little small bowls or saucers and cups and you could collect them to make a full set.

664
00:49:34.876 --> 00:49:36.455
Four or six settings.

665
00:49:36.455 --> 00:49:40.009
Those are the little things that I can remember.

666
00:49:40.009 --> 00:49:47.116
Also . . . And back then it was not a set of dishes like you have now where everything matched.

667
00:49:47.116 --> 00:49:49.485
Things were just whatever you had.

668
00:49:49.485 --> 00:49:54.223
So it . . . I can remember my mother had . . . Dee some blue dishes,

669
00:49:54.223 --> 00:49:59.356
I called my parents by their names, so Dee had some blue dishes.

670
00:49:59.356 --> 00:50:07.253
They weren't the Blue Willow that all of people see now and collect, but she did have some of those.

671
00:50:07.253 --> 00:50:15.545
And a lot of years later when you would be able to go to the grocery stores, all of the ladies,

672
00:50:15.545 --> 00:50:22.652
they collect stamps, and then they could trade those in to get sets of dishes where everything matched.

673
00:50:22.652 --> 00:50:29.760
So there were some green ones with the yellow flowers in them and brown trim with brown flowers.

674
00:50:29.760 --> 00:50:31.339
Those kinds of things.

675
00:50:31.339 --> 00:50:33.313
<v DRAGOON>And what kind of meals?

676
00:50:33.313 --> 00:50:38.841
Like do you cook things that you used to eat when you were young?

677
00:50:38.841 --> 00:50:39.631
<v JONES>Oh, yes.

678
00:50:39.631 --> 00:50:40.026
Yeah.

679
00:50:40.026 --> 00:50:42.000
<v DRAGOON>What kinds of things?

680
00:50:42.000 --> 00:50:42.976
<v JONES>Smothered chicken.

681
00:50:42.976 --> 00:50:46.390
Chicken and okra with sausage and shrimp.

682
00:50:46.390 --> 00:50:49.317
<c.p>Sometimes just okra, sausage, and shrimp.

683
00:50:49.317 --> 00:50:55.659
I don't cook it, but my son has, the potatoes with stew meat.

684
00:50:55.659 --> 00:50:58.585
That was something my mother cooked.

685
00:50:58.585 --> 00:51:02.488
We would have liver with gravy and rice.

686
00:51:02.488 --> 00:51:04.439
Things along that line.

687
00:51:04.439 --> 00:51:05.415
Pork roast.

688
00:51:05.415 --> 00:51:18.098
I used to always cook pork roast on Sundays and have like speckled butter beans with okra or purple hull peas with okra with the roast.

689
00:51:18.098 --> 00:51:22.976
Those were the things that I remember my mother cooking.

690
00:51:22.976 --> 00:51:25.902
For the holidays it was ham.

691
00:51:25.902 --> 00:51:30.293
She always cooked a ham and everybody loved it.

692
00:51:30.293 --> 00:51:35.171
I can cook a ham, but not like she could.

693
00:51:35.171 --> 00:51:38.098
I'm going to be totally honest.

694
00:51:38.098 --> 00:51:42.000
<v DRAGOON>Where did your family go grocery shopping?

695
00:51:42.000 --> 00:51:44.358
<v JONES>We would go to Westlake.

696
00:51:44.358 --> 00:51:50.491
We would go . . . At one time it was a grocery store called Broussard’s.

697
00:51:50.491 --> 00:51:53.321
Then there was a Piggly Wiggly.

698
00:51:53.321 --> 00:51:56.623
Later there . . . Broussard’s became Marcantel’s in Westlake.

699
00:51:56.623 --> 00:51:59.453
Then the Market Basket came in.

700
00:51:59.453 --> 00:52:01.811
We would go to A&amp;P.

701
00:52:01.811 --> 00:52:04.642
That was located on Highway 90.

702
00:52:04.642 --> 00:52:06.057
Then to Weingartens.

703
00:52:06.057 --> 00:52:11.245
So those were the stores that we shopped at a lot.

704
00:52:11.245 --> 00:52:23.038
Oh, and . . . For the holidays we would go to Missies over in Sulphur to get the big roast or the big ham at that time.

705
00:52:23.038 --> 00:52:28.698
<v DRAGOON>Did you ever do any trades with folks locally for produce, or?

706
00:52:28.698 --> 00:52:32.000
<v JONES>No. We didn't have a garden, so.

707
00:52:32.000 --> 00:52:35.302
Now my parents would buy meat deals.

708
00:52:35.302 --> 00:52:42.377
They'd find a butcher shop that would have meat deals and they would do that.

709
00:52:42.377 --> 00:52:47.566
I can remember how excited they were to get a freezer.

710
00:52:47.566 --> 00:52:53.698
A deep freezer to put meat in once they got the meat deals.

711
00:52:53.698 --> 00:52:57.000
<v DRAGOON>Did your family ever go hunting?

712
00:52:57.000 --> 00:52:58.861
<v JONES>Joe did, my dad.

713
00:52:58.861 --> 00:53:00.722
He would go hunting.

714
00:53:00.722 --> 00:53:12.819
He'd go squirrel hunting and he would go over into . . . Hunting in the area where my grandparents lived because it was a lot of wooded area.

715
00:53:12.819 --> 00:53:19.799
So he would . . . kill squirrels and bring those home and my grandmother would cook those.

716
00:53:19.799 --> 00:53:25.382
She'd make, I think it was . . . I think she called it dumplings.

717
00:53:25.382 --> 00:53:27.243
Dirty rice or something.

718
00:53:27.243 --> 00:53:29.569
But she would cook that.

719
00:53:29.569 --> 00:53:31.896
And my husband would go.

720
00:53:31.896 --> 00:53:37.014
He went a couple of times with my dad hunting also.

721
00:53:37.014 --> 00:53:38.875
<v DRAGOON>Did you ever go?

722
00:53:38.875 --> 00:53:46.319
<v JONES>No. I didn't mind the fishing, but I think my dad never would take me hunting.

723
00:53:46.319 --> 00:53:52.368
<v DRAGOON>Where did your family go for medical treatment if you were sick or . . .?

724
00:53:52.368 --> 00:53:56.090
<v JONES>We went to Doctor Smith over in Sulphur.

725
00:53:56.090 --> 00:53:56.556
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

726
00:53:56.556 --> 00:53:58.417
<v JONES>Or to Cal-Cam hospital.

727
00:53:58.417 --> 00:54:04.000
<v DRAGOON>Do you remember there being any healers in the Mossville area?

728
00:54:04.000 --> 00:54:12.772
<v JONES>If there were any they weren't in the Lincoln Heights or the Bel Air subdivision where we were.

729
00:54:12.772 --> 00:54:16.184
<v DRAGOON>Did your family have any home remedies?

730
00:54:16.184 --> 00:54:23.006
<v JONES>Yeah just little things like . . . I don't know if you'd call it a remedy,

731
00:54:23.006 --> 00:54:32.753
but like if we were congested my mother would put a pot of water on the stove and boil it.

732
00:54:32.753 --> 00:54:48.835
Put a tablespoon of Vick's Vapor Rub in that water and let it come to a boil and then that odor was in the house and it would open up a congested nose.

733
00:54:48.835 --> 00:54:57.608
If you had a bad cough rubbing Vick's Vapor Rub on the chest to get that loosened up.

734
00:54:57.608 --> 00:55:02.968
I was trying to think if . . . I can't remember anything else.

735
00:55:02.968 --> 00:55:07.842
Usually she would just get something from the drug store.

736
00:55:07.842 --> 00:55:11.253
<v DRAGOON>How were babies delivered in the area?

737
00:55:11.253 --> 00:55:12.715
<v JONES>At the hospitals.

738
00:55:12.715 --> 00:55:14.177
<v DRAGOON>At the hospital.

739
00:55:14.177 --> 00:55:15.152
<v JONES>Yeah. [Agrees].

740
00:55:15.152 --> 00:55:21.000
<v DRAGOON>What kinds of shops do you remember in the Mossville area?

741
00:55:21.000 --> 00:55:28.706
<v JONES>Now in our . . . In the Lincoln Heights and Bel Air area we had like just eventually a little grocery store came in,

742
00:55:28.706 --> 00:55:32.559
but for clothing we'd have to go to Westlake or Sulphur.

743
00:55:32.559 --> 00:55:39.215
Bradley's in Westlake was one store, and then West Brothers in Sulphur, but nothing actually there in our community.

744
00:55:39.215 --> 00:55:43.418
<v DRAGOON>And you said you would sew your own clothes as well sometimes?

745
00:55:43.418 --> 00:55:44.819
<v JONES>Yeah I would sew.

746
00:55:44.819 --> 00:55:50.774
Yeah but most of the time my dad would buy . . . Get money for me to buy clothes.

747
00:55:50.774 --> 00:55:57.429
But they didn't deny me the opportunity to do what the other kids were doing too and making them.

748
00:55:57.429 --> 00:55:59.531
<v DRAGOON>Did you have a sewing machine?

749
00:55:59.531 --> 00:56:00.582
<v JONES>Yes I did.

750
00:56:00.582 --> 00:56:02.684
I had a Singer sewing machine.

751
00:56:02.684 --> 00:56:11.441
Dee had an old machine and after she saw that I was going to stick with it, then she got a new machine for me.

752
00:56:11.441 --> 00:56:19.847
Then the lady next door, when she was teaching her girls to sew, between she and my mom they taught me how to sew.

753
00:56:19.847 --> 00:56:23.000
<v DRAGOON>Where did she get the old sewing machine?

754
00:56:23.000 --> 00:56:26.143
<v JONES>I don't know where she got it from.

755
00:56:26.143 --> 00:56:33.607
Because I can remember my grandmother and her sisters making quilts, but my grandmother didn't have a sewing machine.

756
00:56:33.607 --> 00:56:39.893
But her sister, she had one of the old treadle . . . Where you do with the foot.

757
00:56:39.893 --> 00:56:48.143
But I don't ever recall my grandmother having a sewing machine, so I don't know where my mom learned to sew.

758
00:56:48.143 --> 00:56:53.643
<v DRAGOON>What did you do for fun when you were a teenager with your friends?

759
00:56:53.643 --> 00:56:57.571
I know you mentioned walking around with your record player.

760
00:56:57.571 --> 00:57:03.857
<v JONES>Yeah that was the big thing. [laughs] That was the big thing, and just listening to

761
00:57:03.857 --> 00:57:04.643
<c.p>the radio.

762
00:57:04.643 --> 00:57:12.500
We'd get together, get out in the backyard and somebody would get an extension cord and turn the radio on.

763
00:57:12.500 --> 00:57:17.607
Bring it out as far as we could and we'd listen to music.

764
00:57:17.607 --> 00:57:23.500
Or we'd sit in the house and talk on the telephone or watch different shows.

765
00:57:23.500 --> 00:57:34.500
Every week we had something . . . At one time it was like Star Trek or something that we would sit and watch and then everybody talk about it later.

766
00:57:34.500 --> 00:57:37.643
<v DRAGOON>Did you ever go to see live music?

767
00:57:37.643 --> 00:57:40.000
<v JONES>Not until I was grown.

768
00:57:40.000 --> 00:57:43.800
<v DRAGOON>And you mentioned the dances that happened once in a while?

769
00:57:43.800 --> 00:57:49.673
<v JONES>Yes, in the summer when they would have the teen town and we would go to that.

770
00:57:49.673 --> 00:57:50.364
<v DRAGOON>Teen town.

771
00:57:50.364 --> 00:57:50.709
<v JONES>[agrees]

772
00:57:50.709 --> 00:57:52.091
<v DRAGOON>Where was that held?

773
00:57:52.091 --> 00:58:00.036
<v JONES>That was held at the Rigmaiden Center and it was like I said we had . . . His name was Bubba Lutcher we called him.

774
00:58:00.036 --> 00:58:06.600
He'd be on the radio, but on Thursdays he would come over to Mossville and they'd play the records.

775
00:58:06.600 --> 00:58:09.709
It was like a . . . Basically like a record hop.

776
00:58:09.709 --> 00:58:10.055
We'd

777
00:58:10.055 --> 00:58:11.091
<c.p>go to that.

778
00:58:11.091 --> 00:58:16.618
Then we had a lady Ms. Reesa Mae, she had what they called a little canteen.

779
00:58:16.618 --> 00:58:24.909
Now I didn't get to go to that often because Dee wouldn't let me, but on Sundays a lot of kids would go there.

780
00:58:24.909 --> 00:58:32.509
And after the football games you could go and buy cookies and pickles and listen to . . . They had a jukebox in there.

781
00:58:32.509 --> 00:58:37.000
You put your quarter in and everybody dance and things like that.

782
00:58:37.000 --> 00:58:40.223
<v DRAGOON>But Dee didn't want you to go to the canteens? [laughs]

783
00:58:40.223 --> 00:58:42.860
<v JONES>No. I didn't get to go to those often.

784
00:58:42.860 --> 00:58:43.739
<v DRAGOON>Why is that?

785
00:58:43.739 --> 00:58:50.185
<v JONES>She just didn't think that I should be going because we'd have to walk back and it was a couple of blocks.

786
00:58:50.185 --> 00:58:54.287
We didn't have street lights so she just didn't think it was really safe.

787
00:58:54.287 --> 00:58:56.924
She said, “You know, you don't know who’s watching.”

788
00:58:56.924 --> 00:59:01.025
So . . . And then she didn't always have a car to come pick me up.

789
00:59:01.025 --> 00:59:07.178
Even if I'd tell her I was going to walk back with a group of girls she'd go, "It's not safe.”

790
00:59:07.178 --> 00:59:11.280
She was like, “They grab one they can grab more than one of y'all."

791
00:59:11.280 --> 00:59:12.159
She said, "Nope."

792
00:59:12.159 --> 00:59:14.503
So I didn't get to do that often.

793
00:59:14.503 --> 00:59:17.433
I was kind of sheltered on some of those things.

794
00:59:17.433 --> 00:59:23.000
I didn't get to go to teen town every time when they were held on Thursday nights either.

795
00:59:23.000 --> 00:59:25.964
<v DRAGOON>Because your . . . Because Dee was nervous about . . .

796
00:59:25.964 --> 00:59:26.387
<v JONES>Yeah.

797
00:59:26.387 --> 00:59:27.658
<v DRAGOON>Maybe unsafe activity?

798
00:59:27.658 --> 00:59:28.081
<v JONES>Yeah.

799
00:59:28.081 --> 00:59:36.126
Now see, and if we went to teen town that was a long ways to walk back at night.

800
00:59:36.126 --> 00:59:38.667
Especially just a bunch of girls.

801
00:59:38.667 --> 00:59:44.171
So I didn't . . . Like I said I get to do that often, either.

802
00:59:44.171 --> 00:59:56.027
She was a little more comfortable if she could drop me off and give me a designated time as to when she was going to pick me up,

803
00:59:56.027 --> 00:59:58.991
or who would be picking me up.

804
00:59:58.991 --> 00:59:59.838
<v DRAGOON>Let’s see.

805
00:59:59.838 --> 01:00:00.261
Okay.

806
01:00:00.261 --> 01:00:04.919
Do you remember . . . What do you remember about the Paradise Club?

807
01:00:04.919 --> 01:00:05.342
Anything?

808
01:00:05.342 --> 01:00:06.189
<v JONES>Oh, yes.

809
01:00:06.189 --> 01:00:08.730
I had my wedding reception there.

810
01:00:08.730 --> 01:00:10.000
<v DRAGOON>Oh, wow.

811
01:00:10.000 --> 01:00:19.794
<v JONES>Yeah later that night we had just the young people went up to the Paradise and I don't remember attending any of the dances there,

812
01:00:19.794 --> 01:00:22.536
but there was some really good performers.

813
01:00:22.536 --> 01:00:24.495
James Brown had been there.

814
01:00:24.495 --> 01:00:26.062
I heard Tyron Davis.

815
01:00:26.062 --> 01:00:33.505
Just a lot of different performers that had gone there, and you would always hear somebody talking about it.

816
01:00:33.505 --> 01:00:39.381
Like I said, but I knew I was under age so that was another thing.

817
01:00:39.381 --> 01:00:49.567
And see by the community being so small you better not show up an underage because they would go, "What are you doing here young lady?

818
01:00:49.567 --> 01:00:51.918
Does your mama know you're here?

819
01:00:51.918 --> 01:00:54.660
Or does your daddy know you're here?

820
01:00:54.660 --> 01:00:57.794
Wait a little let me go call them."

821
01:00:57.794 --> 01:01:05.629
So to avoid from getting further troubles with Dee I knew it was best not to head in that direction.

822
01:01:05.629 --> 01:01:09.546
But I can remember it was two-story . . . A two-story club.

823
01:01:09.546 --> 01:01:11.113
There was a bar

824
01:01:11.113 --> 01:01:11.505
<c.p>downstairs.

825
01:01:11.505 --> 01:01:19.340
Like I said, it was . . . Some dances I would hear people talk about it, but I never got to go.

826
01:01:19.340 --> 01:01:22.866
<v DRAGOON>It sounds like it had kind of a reputation.

827
01:01:22.866 --> 01:01:23.649
<v JONES>It did.

828
01:01:23.649 --> 01:01:24.433
<v DRAGOON>Around town.

829
01:01:24.433 --> 01:01:26.000
Why is that?

830
01:01:26.000 --> 01:01:27.142
<v JONES>I don't know.

831
01:01:27.142 --> 01:01:28.285
I don't know.

832
01:01:28.285 --> 01:01:32.092
Like I said, I knew the Montgomerys owned the club.

833
01:01:32.092 --> 01:01:35.519
I went to school with the younger son Alvin,

834
01:01:35.519 --> 01:01:44.657
but . . . and I knew Mr. Valery and my dad would talk all the time and Joe would go to . . . He'd go to the clubs.

835
01:01:44.657 --> 01:01:45.799
Now Dee didn't.

836
01:01:45.799 --> 01:01:54.937
She stayed at home, but he'd go meet his buddies and they'd go there and they'd sit around talking and drink some and whatever.

837
01:01:54.937 --> 01:01:56.841
Then they would come back.

838
01:01:56.841 --> 01:01:58.364
He'd come back home.

839
01:01:58.364 --> 01:02:01.410
But I just knew I wasn't allowed there.

840
01:02:01.410 --> 01:02:09.025
<v DRAGOON>Did a lot of folks . . . Was it mostly men who would go to the Paradise Club or was it like . . .?

841
01:02:09.025 --> 01:02:12.071
<v JONES>There were some ladies too that would go.

842
01:02:12.071 --> 01:02:14.356
Men and women that would go.

843
01:02:14.356 --> 01:02:14.736
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

844
01:02:14.736 --> 01:02:15.117
<v JONES>Yeah.

845
01:02:15.117 --> 01:02:15.498
Couples.

846
01:02:15.498 --> 01:02:18.925
There were a lot of couples that would go.

847
01:02:18.925 --> 01:02:19.305
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

848
01:02:19.305 --> 01:02:23.494
<v JONES>And especially when it was a dance going to be held.

849
01:02:23.494 --> 01:02:29.967
They would call . . . Different ones would call and ask Dee if they were going to be going,

850
01:02:29.967 --> 01:02:36.820
but she would always say no. She wasn't going by herself and if . . . Especially if Joe was gone.

851
01:02:36.820 --> 01:02:40.628
I don't remember them going there to any dances together.

852
01:02:40.628 --> 01:02:44.816
Joe probably did if he was on his way back in,

853
01:02:44.816 --> 01:02:50.527
but I just . . . She wasn't much of a person to go to clubs and such.

854
01:02:50.527 --> 01:02:54.335
We had another little club that was near our house.

855
01:02:54.335 --> 01:02:57.000
It was called the Cowboy Inn.

856
01:02:57.000 --> 01:02:57.697
<v DRAGOON>The Cowboy?

857
01:02:57.697 --> 01:02:58.394
<v JONES>Cowboy Inn.

858
01:02:58.394 --> 01:02:58.743
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

859
01:02:58.743 --> 01:02:59.091
Wow.

860
01:02:59.091 --> 01:02:59.440
<v JONES>Yeah.

861
01:02:59.440 --> 01:03:00.834
<v DRAGOON>Where was that located?

862
01:03:00.834 --> 01:03:03.274
<v JONES>It was in the Lincoln Heights area.

863
01:03:03.274 --> 01:03:06.411
Bel Air, Lincoln Heights, it was on Fifth Avenue.

864
01:03:06.411 --> 01:03:09.200
It was owned and run by Peter DeVille.

865
01:03:09.200 --> 01:03:11.291
It was a little small club.

866
01:03:11.291 --> 01:03:12.686
It was nowhere near

867
01:03:12.686 --> 01:03:16.869
<c.p>the Paradise, and they never had performers or anybody to go there.

868
01:03:16.869 --> 01:03:24.189
It was I think more of a place where you could just go and sit down and get drinks and such.

869
01:03:24.189 --> 01:03:29.417
I remember going . . . I was in a wedding and the bridal party was going there.

870
01:03:29.417 --> 01:03:37.434
And I showed up and there was a lady sitting there and I'll never forget she said, "Brenda, what are you doing here?

871
01:03:37.434 --> 01:03:39.526
Does your grandmother know you're here?"

872
01:03:39.526 --> 01:03:41.966
And I was like, oh my gosh.

873
01:03:41.966 --> 01:03:45.800
So I knew then that it was over with for me.

874
01:03:45.800 --> 01:03:54.166
We all had to leave because once the owner recognized me he was like, "Oh no you're not old enough to be in here.

875
01:03:54.166 --> 01:03:55.211
See you later."

876
01:03:55.211 --> 01:03:58.000
So then we all had to leave.

877
01:03:58.000 --> 01:04:02.079
<v DRAGOON>Sounds like it was kind of hard to get away with stuff.

878
01:04:02.079 --> 01:04:05.138
<v JONES>Yeah you weren't going to get away with much.

879
01:04:05.138 --> 01:04:11.257
Depending upon who you were, you were not going to get away with much in that little community.

880
01:04:11.257 --> 01:04:20.435
They would watch . . . And it was one of those things where it was like it takes a village to raise a child and that's what they did.

881
01:04:20.435 --> 01:04:21.455
They’d look out.

882
01:04:21.455 --> 01:04:26.553
If somebody's child didn't go to school and the parents would be looking for them,

883
01:04:26.553 --> 01:04:31.992
you couldn't think you were going to hide out at nobody's house like children do nowadays.

884
01:04:31.992 --> 01:04:36.751
They don't go to school, but somebody will let them hangout at their house.

885
01:04:36.751 --> 01:04:38.791
They pass by and they're like,

886
01:04:38.791 --> 01:04:48.308
"Hey I'm looking for so and so did you see him he was supposed to go to school but I see his books are still on the corner."

887
01:04:48.308 --> 01:04:54.087
So it was . . . They’d start looking, "No, I didn't see him, but I will let you know."

888
01:04:54.087 --> 01:04:59.186
And trust me if they saw you they’d go, "Hey your mama's looking for you."

889
01:04:59.186 --> 01:05:04.625
So, but I told them once we left Mossville High School and started going to Westlake,

890
01:05:04.625 --> 01:05:14.822
it was like we had a job because the bus picked us up at six forty-five in the mornings and we didn't get back until four thirty in the evenings.

891
01:05:14.822 --> 01:05:19.581
So the parents had a real good chance keeping tab on the younger children.

892
01:05:19.581 --> 01:05:22.980
<v DRAGOON>What was it like going to Westlake for high school?

893
01:05:22.980 --> 01:05:24.000
Was it . . .?

894
01:05:24.000 --> 01:05:25.644
<v JONES>It was totally different.

895
01:05:25.644 --> 01:05:27.288
<v DRAGOON>Yeah was it a . . .

896
01:05:27.288 --> 01:05:28.110
<v JONES>Than being . . .

897
01:05:28.110 --> 01:05:28.932
<v DRAGOON>Bigger school?

898
01:05:28.932 --> 01:05:30.575
<v JONES>It was a . . . Yeah.

899
01:05:30.575 --> 01:05:32.630
It was a bigger school.

900
01:05:32.630 --> 01:05:35.096
Things were just done totally different.

901
01:05:35.096 --> 01:05:39.205
We met a lot of prejudice once we got there.

902
01:05:39.205 --> 01:05:52.356
I can remember the police had to be called out to be on campus with us because of the problems that we had the first year that we were in attendance there.

903
01:05:52.356 --> 01:05:54.000
<v DRAGOON>What was happening?

904
01:05:54.000 --> 01:06:03.509
<v JONES>It was a lot of fights and a lot of the parents didn't want the children from . . . Us from Mossville to attend the school there.

905
01:06:03.509 --> 01:06:08.454
And I think more the problems stem from the parents than the children.

906
01:06:08.454 --> 01:06:15.681
The children were probably already afraid, but then when the parents came into the school and would tell you,

907
01:06:15.681 --> 01:06:20.245
"Don't get next to my child you're going to fade on them."

908
01:06:20.245 --> 01:06:23.288
Or they’d push you over on the side.

909
01:06:23.288 --> 01:06:29.374
And so it just got to the point where they had to call the police in.

910
01:06:29.374 --> 01:06:35.460
They had to come into the school and actually be in the halls when we would

911
01:06:35.460 --> 01:06:38.123
<c.p>be changing classes and in the cafeteria.

912
01:06:38.123 --> 01:06:39.264
That went on.

913
01:06:39.264 --> 01:06:49.153
And then the principal that was there when we first started, he ended up having a heart attack and they had to bring somebody else in.

914
01:06:49.153 --> 01:06:56.000
They started trying to get some improvements done in regards to the communication and cooperation between everybody.

915
01:06:56.000 --> 01:06:58.524
I remember one morning arriving at school.

916
01:06:58.524 --> 01:07:02.490
I had missed the bus and I was in serious trouble.

917
01:07:02.490 --> 01:07:08.981
Dee had taken me to school and she dropped me off and I noticed it was real strange.

918
01:07:08.981 --> 01:07:13.308
I didn't see anybody that I normally rode the bus with outside.

919
01:07:13.308 --> 01:07:20.159
And the assistant principal was outside and he told me to come in . . . Me and another guy Payton Smith.

920
01:07:20.159 --> 01:07:32.058
And he brought us inside the school and he led us to the auditorium and that's where I saw the rest of the kids that would ride the bus from Mossville in there.

921
01:07:32.058 --> 01:07:36.024
But before we got in, there were parents that were outside.

922
01:07:36.024 --> 01:07:43.957
They had ropes with . . . And they were shaking them at Payton and I and saying, "This is for your kind of people."

923
01:07:43.957 --> 01:07:45.760
So what do you do?

924
01:07:45.760 --> 01:07:52.971
You kind of look around and you the only two black people out there so you know you're . . . It's like,

925
01:07:52.971 --> 01:07:55.135
"Oh my, what's going to happen?"

926
01:07:55.135 --> 01:08:01.625
So it's . . . Then the parents had gotten phone calls and they had to come and pick us up.

927
01:08:01.625 --> 01:08:07.034
And so it was just . . . It was real stressful the first year of going there.

928
01:08:07.034 --> 01:08:11.000
<v DRAGOON>Was that the first year of integration of that school?

929
01:08:11.000 --> 01:08:16.743
<v JONES>No there had been just a few black students that had been attending the school,

930
01:08:16.743 --> 01:08:22.486
but this was the first time buses were rolling in bringing students into the school.

931
01:08:22.486 --> 01:08:24.784
It was a lot of fights.

932
01:08:24.784 --> 01:08:38.568
It was just something that I had never experienced because I had never been taught that there was a difference in people because Joe . . . His coworkers would always come by the house and they would talk.

933
01:08:38.568 --> 01:08:43.928
And they would invite us over to their homes and we never had any

934
01:08:43.928 --> 01:08:44.311
<c.p>problems.

935
01:08:44.311 --> 01:08:50.437
So when I get there and see this happening, it was a rude awakening for me.

936
01:08:50.437 --> 01:08:53.117
I didn't know just what to expect.

937
01:08:53.117 --> 01:08:55.414
<v DRAGOON>Were Joe's coworkers mostly white folks?

938
01:08:55.414 --> 01:08:55.797
<v JONES>Yes.

939
01:08:55.797 --> 01:08:59.243
Yeah and even the people that owned the company,

940
01:08:59.243 --> 01:09:06.518
they would invite him over to their house and he would bring us and we'd sit down and talk.

941
01:09:06.518 --> 01:09:10.347
And it was just never any prejudice that was shown.

942
01:09:10.347 --> 01:09:19.153
So when I got over there and started seeing this kind of stuff going on it was just . . . It wasn't what I expected.

943
01:09:19.153 --> 01:09:26.428
I just thought the people were really rude for some of the things that were being done and said.

944
01:09:26.428 --> 01:09:27.577
There's no reason.

945
01:09:27.577 --> 01:09:33.703
<v DRAGOON>Had any adults in your life talked to you about what you were about to face?

946
01:09:33.703 --> 01:09:34.851
Did people know?

947
01:09:34.851 --> 01:09:36.000
Did you . . . ?

948
01:09:36.000 --> 01:09:41.980
<v JONES>No they never said anything and if our parents did they didn't say anything about it.

949
01:09:41.980 --> 01:09:44.970
I mean the family next door to me,

950
01:09:44.970 --> 01:09:52.071
they had had a son that had attended high school there and had graduated, and they never said anything.

951
01:09:52.071 --> 01:09:58.424
And then their children were starting to go to Westlake at the same time I was going.

952
01:09:58.424 --> 01:10:03.657
And there was another family . . . Several other families, had children that were going there.

953
01:10:03.657 --> 01:10:06.273
We just never heard anything about it.

954
01:10:06.273 --> 01:10:13.000
<v DRAGOON>The day that you got to school late and . . . It was mostly Mossville kids in the auditorium?

955
01:10:13.000 --> 01:10:16.774
<v JONES>Yes it was all the Mossville kids in the auditorium.

956
01:10:16.774 --> 01:10:22.811
<v DRAGOON>And they were . . . and that was . . . was that the . . . Like towards the beginning of the year?

957
01:10:22.811 --> 01:10:23.943
<v JONES>Yes it was.

958
01:10:23.943 --> 01:10:26.962
<v DRAGOON>It wasn't the first day of school though?

959
01:10:26.962 --> 01:10:29.981
<v JONES>No, it wasn't the first day of school.

960
01:10:29.981 --> 01:10:30.358
No.

961
01:10:30.358 --> 01:10:30.736
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

962
01:10:30.736 --> 01:10:40.170
<v JONES>No, because by the time we went back to school after Labor Day is when we had the police that had to be on campus.

963
01:10:40.170 --> 01:10:46.962
Because see, back then we started school in August so it was prior to the Labor Day holiday.

964
01:10:46.962 --> 01:10:53.000
Then like I said that's when we . . . It was like almost right off the bat.

965
01:10:53.000 --> 01:10:54.731
<v DRAGOON>And these were parents . . .

966
01:10:54.731 --> 01:10:55.164
<v JONES>Yes.

967
01:10:55.164 --> 01:10:56.030
<v DRAGOON>Protesting outside?

968
01:10:56.030 --> 01:10:58.627
And was that happening every day?

969
01:10:58.627 --> 01:10:59.060
<v JONES>Yes.

970
01:10:59.060 --> 01:11:01.224
You always had parents there.

971
01:11:01.224 --> 01:11:07.284
When the buses would pull up you would see cars with parents there already.

972
01:11:07.284 --> 01:11:15.075
At first I didn't pay them any attention because I thought they were just dropping their children off,

973
01:11:15.075 --> 01:11:22.433
but then I noticed more and more of them were staying and coming into the school itself.

974
01:11:22.433 --> 01:11:33.254
And it . . . A lot of it probably could have been avoided if they would have been told that they were not allowed in the school.

975
01:11:33.254 --> 01:11:35.851
Just let the kids handle it.

976
01:11:35.851 --> 01:11:46.239
I think it probably would have worked out a lot sooner and a lot smoother, but by the parents being there it got ugly.

977
01:11:46.239 --> 01:11:51.000
<v DRAGOON>What was a typical day at Westlake like for you?

978
01:11:51.000 --> 01:12:00.898
<v JONES>Well once we'd get there we'd get there so early the school wasn't open and we’d just be standing outside until the school actually opened up.

979
01:12:00.898 --> 01:12:03.562
<v DRAGOON>How long would you be waiting for?

980
01:12:03.562 --> 01:12:13.841
<v JONES>Gosh, the bus would pick us up at like a quarter to . . . Like I said a quarter to seven and we'd get there a little after seven,

981
01:12:13.841 --> 01:12:19.170
but they wouldn't open up until . . . because about twenty minutes to eight or so.

982
01:12:19.170 --> 01:12:24.500
Just before . . . A few minutes before it was time for the bell to ring.

983
01:12:24.500 --> 01:12:35.540
Once you got in you had to get to your lockers and get to your homeroom and be there for roll call and that was just a typical day.

984
01:12:35.540 --> 01:12:38.966
And we'd be the last students to leave campus.

985
01:12:38.966 --> 01:12:45.057
Everybody else, except for the kids that were participating in sports, everybody else would be gone.

986
01:12:45.057 --> 01:12:48.102
We'd be waiting on our bus to come.

987
01:12:48.102 --> 01:12:49.244
<v DRAGOON>Why is that?

988
01:12:49.244 --> 01:12:53.051
<v JONES>Because they had so many areas to pick up kids.

989
01:12:53.051 --> 01:12:58.000
<v DRAGOON>And how long were the police at your school do you think?

990
01:12:58.000 --> 01:13:09.692
<v JONES>I'd say for a couple weeks they were there just to try to get it . . . Get everything settled down.

991
01:13:09.692 --> 01:13:14.000
<v DRAGOON>How did the teachers treat you?

992
01:13:14.000 --> 01:13:19.532
<v JONES>They basically just checked roll and got your assignments, and that was it.

993
01:13:19.532 --> 01:13:24.638
They didn't have any interaction with the students that I can recall.

994
01:13:24.638 --> 01:13:25.915
Not at first.

995
01:13:25.915 --> 01:13:36.553
And then later I guess it was just to see what type of students we were going to be and who was interested in learning.

996
01:13:36.553 --> 01:13:44.213
And then as the years went by, then you could see a difference in some of the teachers.

997
01:13:44.213 --> 01:13:54.851
A lot of the teachers at Westlake High they had children of their own that were in attendance there because they lived in Westlake, also.

998
01:13:54.851 --> 01:13:58.681
<v DRAGOON>I know you were pretty young in the sixties.

999
01:13:58.681 --> 01:13:59.106
<v JONES>Yes.

1000
01:13:59.106 --> 01:14:04.638
<v DRAGOON>But do you remember what that time was like during the Civil Rights

1001
01:14:04.638 --> 01:14:05.064
<c.p>Movement?

1002
01:14:05.064 --> 01:14:11.872
Did your parents tell you stories of that time, or did you talk about it much?

1003
01:14:11.872 --> 01:14:13.149
<v JONES>No. They didn't.

1004
01:14:13.149 --> 01:14:14.000
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

1005
01:14:14.000 --> 01:14:24.569
<v JONES>No. Now every now and then I would hear Joe talk about areas that he would go to drive through, sometimes he'd run into problems.

1006
01:14:24.569 --> 01:14:35.984
One of the things I can always remember him talking about was when he would go places you had . . . It was segregated as to colored for eating.

1007
01:14:35.984 --> 01:14:41.480
So was it . . . Over in Sulphur when you go to the doctor’s office.

1008
01:14:41.480 --> 01:14:47.821
You had bathrooms that were marked for colored only in restaurants, at the doctor's office.

1009
01:14:47.821 --> 01:14:58.813
So and then . . . It stayed like that for years and then some of the doctors just shut it down and said everybody come into one area.

1010
01:14:58.813 --> 01:15:06.000
It wasn't going to be only one group in this area and another group over here.

1011
01:15:06.000 --> 01:15:09.107
<v DRAGOON>When did you notice that change start happening?

1012
01:15:09.107 --> 01:15:10.272
<v JONES>In the seventies.

1013
01:15:10.272 --> 01:15:16.097
I noticed the doctor that I would go to over in Sulphur, Doctor Smith's office,

1014
01:15:16.097 --> 01:15:21.534
they would . . . They didn't have a sign on the door that said “colored only.”

1015
01:15:21.534 --> 01:15:35.903
One day I went there and I didn't see the sign and I went in and I noticed it was just a couple of people sitting there and so one of the ladies came and she said,

1016
01:15:35.903 --> 01:15:42.893
"Do you want to come sit in the front?' And I looked at her and I was like,

1017
01:15:42.893 --> 01:15:47.942
"No." And she said, "You don't have to sit back here you know."

1018
01:15:47.942 --> 01:15:54.155
She said, "It's no sign on the door; you're welcome to sit wherever you want to."

1019
01:15:54.155 --> 01:15:56.485
So that was a little different.

1020
01:15:56.485 --> 01:16:03.087
But like I said in Westlake you would see a lot of that if there was a

1021
01:16:03.087 --> 01:16:07.748
<c.p>bathroom, a restroom, it would have a sign on it saying “colored.”

1022
01:16:07.748 --> 01:16:20.951
Or if you went to get something to eat . . . If you went to pick up food . . . Drive for pick up food you'd see the signs where you could only go in at certain doors.

1023
01:16:20.951 --> 01:16:26.000
<v DRAGOON>What kinds of stories did your father tell about driving through places?

1024
01:16:26.000 --> 01:16:34.207
<v JONES>He would say some areas he would get to . . . And it was getting into the late sixties,

1025
01:16:34.207 --> 01:16:40.966
like after Martin Luther King had been killed and President Kennedy had been killed,

1026
01:16:40.966 --> 01:16:52.069
and he would say that there were certain areas like in Mississippi and Alabama what still had the signs up saying “colored only.”

1027
01:16:52.069 --> 01:16:56.414
Water fountains that had “colored only” displayed on them.

1028
01:16:56.414 --> 01:17:08.966
So he would say it didn't matter to him because he knew he wasn't going to be allowed to go into where it said “whites only.”

1029
01:17:08.966 --> 01:17:22.483
So he said as long as he got his food and was able to get back on the road that was the only thing that mattered to him.

1030
01:17:22.483 --> 01:17:33.586
He said the only thing he couldn't understand, why you would say you didn't want black people to go into a certain area,

1031
01:17:33.586 --> 01:17:38.897
but you're allowing them to cook the food that you're eating.

1032
01:17:38.897 --> 01:17:43.724
He said, "That's where you should have really been worried."

1033
01:17:43.724 --> 01:17:46.621
If anything was going to happen.

1034
01:17:46.621 --> 01:17:51.931
<v DRAGOON>And . . . Oh I had a question that just escaped my mind.

1035
01:17:51.931 --> 01:17:54.828
What materials was your father delivering?

1036
01:17:54.828 --> 01:17:59.172
<v JONES>For a while he drove and he delivered steps.

1037
01:17:59.172 --> 01:18:00.138
Cement steps.

1038
01:18:00.138 --> 01:18:04.966
And he would load them on and off the trucks.

1039
01:18:04.966 --> 01:18:16.552
Then later had went to work for a company that had the wood that was used to make the roads for the oil field.

1040
01:18:16.552 --> 01:18:19.448
The pallets that they would use.

1041
01:18:19.448 --> 01:18:20.414
<v DRAGOON>Oh, okay.

1042
01:18:20.414 --> 01:18:26.690
<v JONES>And then in the mid-sixties he was driving for Cesco and then BFI.

1043
01:18:26.690 --> 01:18:28.621
He was hauling chemicals.

1044
01:18:28.621 --> 01:18:32.000
He was a chemical truck driver.

1045
01:18:32.000 --> 01:18:33.009
<v DRAGOON>Let's see.

1046
01:18:33.009 --> 01:18:39.065
What do you remember about any political activism in or around Mossville?

1047
01:18:39.065 --> 01:18:41.589
<v JONES>I don't really remember any.

1048
01:18:41.589 --> 01:18:56.224
I know you'd hear people coming around when it was getting close to election time . . . Talking to my parents about going to vote and to vote for this person.

1049
01:18:56.224 --> 01:19:06.318
And they would just kind of say why they thought that person would be good to be elected into office.

1050
01:19:06.318 --> 01:19:15.402
But any of . . . Any problems or anything like that my parents would have kept it away from me.

1051
01:19:15.402 --> 01:19:19.439
They wouldn't have let me hear about it.

1052
01:19:19.439 --> 01:19:26.000
<v DRAGOON>And who were the community leaders that folks kind of looked to?

1053
01:19:26.000 --> 01:19:41.000
<v JONES>I would . . . I remember James Rigmaiden would come by and he would talk to my parents because he and my mother had gone to school together.

1054
01:19:41.000 --> 01:19:52.538
And then LaSalle Williams from the community, and then there was Kenneth Lee, he worked with the sheriff's department, also.

1055
01:19:52.538 --> 01:19:58.885
I'm trying to think . . . That's about all that I can remember.

1056
01:19:58.885 --> 01:19:59.462
<v DRAGOON>Sure.

1057
01:19:59.462 --> 01:20:06.962
What do you remember about the refineries around where you were growing up?

1058
01:20:06.962 --> 01:20:14.462
<v JONES>I remember like sometimes we would have real bad odors we would smell.

1059
01:20:14.462 --> 01:20:31.192
Especially if we were getting ready to get on the I-10 bridge because where Olin was and it would smell just like Clorox from there because they made ammonia.

1060
01:20:31.192 --> 01:20:43.885
Then we lived two blocks from the VCM plant, which is now I think a part of Axial due to the merger.

1061
01:20:43.885 --> 01:20:57.154
And sometimes there would be explosions and we would feel our homes shake even from as far away as the Cities Service Refinery.

1062
01:20:57.154 --> 01:21:11.000
I remember panes rattling in our homes because they were older homes, so of course you could feel the vibrations of the homes.

1063
01:21:11.000 --> 01:21:18.424
A lot of people would complain about the plant having exploded the night before and windows being broken out,

1064
01:21:18.424 --> 01:21:23.503
but it was a time back then you didn't hear of the lawsuits.

1065
01:21:23.503 --> 01:21:37.570
Nobody . . . I don't ever remember anybody calling saying they called one of the plants and told them about having lost windows and needing to have somebody come out to inspect their homes or anything like that.

1066
01:21:37.570 --> 01:21:50.854
I remember being at Westlake High and they closed the school because there had been something that had happened at the VCM plant and they were trying to get all the students back home.

1067
01:21:50.854 --> 01:21:57.887
So the buses were there, picked us up, and took us all back home and school was closed.

1068
01:21:57.887 --> 01:22:04.530
I think for the . . . I know for the rest of that day, and maybe the following day.

1069
01:22:04.530 --> 01:22:10.000
Those were just some of the things that I can remember going on.

1070
01:22:10.000 --> 01:22:20.824
<v DRAGOON>Did you hear people like, talk about being worried about like, poisons being released into the air, or into the water, or the ground?

1071
01:22:20.824 --> 01:22:22.176
Were people talking . . .?

1072
01:22:22.176 --> 01:22:24.882
<v JONES>Not while I was living there.

1073
01:22:24.882 --> 01:22:25.333
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

1074
01:22:25.333 --> 01:22:28.490
<v JONES>A lot of it came up later,

1075
01:22:28.490 --> 01:22:37.510
but while I was living there I didn't hear of it because more of the people were my parents' age,

1076
01:22:37.510 --> 01:22:43.824
but the younger people they were more tuned into what was going on environmentally.

1077
01:22:43.824 --> 01:22:51.039
They took a different look at things than what I would say when my parents did.

1078
01:22:51.039 --> 01:22:56.000
<v DRAGOON>How have the refineries surrounding Mossville changed in your lifetime?

1079
01:22:56.000 --> 01:23:01.079
<v JONES>I think they've become . . . Tried to become more community oriented.

1080
01:23:01.079 --> 01:23:06.667
Get buffer zones between their facilities and where homes were located.

1081
01:23:06.667 --> 01:23:08.698
Where people were living.

1082
01:23:08.698 --> 01:23:20.889
<v DRAGOON>And what do you think your . . . What was your view and your family's views and maybe the community's view of Condea Vista and Conoco?

1083
01:23:20.889 --> 01:23:31.556
<v JONES>Like I said my mom was just so mild mannered she didn't say anything one way or the other about it,

1084
01:23:31.556 --> 01:23:43.238
but I don't remember when VCM was constructed, but I'm sure it had to be it was built while we were living there.

1085
01:23:43.238 --> 01:23:47.810
Now the . . . Conoco . . . Well it's now the Phillips Refinery,

1086
01:23:47.810 --> 01:23:56.444
it was there before the community was built because I think it was built in the thirties.

1087
01:23:56.444 --> 01:23:59.492
<v DRAGOON>VCM was built in the thirties?

1088
01:23:59.492 --> 01:24:06.095
<v JONES>No, VCM would have been built sometime in the sixties I would say.

1089
01:24:06.095 --> 01:24:06.603
<v DRAGOON>Sixties.

1090
01:24:06.603 --> 01:24:18.794
<v JONES>[agrees] Yeah because it was the Phillips well it was the old Conoco Refinery, and then the Conoco Chemical Plant, which is now Sasol.

1091
01:24:18.794 --> 01:24:22.857
And then the VCM . . . The old VCM plant,

1092
01:24:22.857 --> 01:24:30.984
it was originally a part of the chemical plant, but it is now part of Axial.

1093
01:24:30.984 --> 01:24:32.000
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

1094
01:24:32.000 --> 01:24:37.538
<v JONES>But like I say, the refinery was built like in the thirties.

1095
01:24:37.538 --> 01:24:39.846
<v DRAGOON>How often would explosions happen?

1096
01:24:39.846 --> 01:24:41.231
<v JONES>Not that often.

1097
01:24:41.231 --> 01:24:43.538
Not that I can remember.

1098
01:24:43.538 --> 01:24:51.385
And it was . . . Most of the time it was at night like when we'd be in bed.

1099
01:24:51.385 --> 01:24:55.538
<v DRAGOON>And was that . . . How did you react to that?

1100
01:24:55.538 --> 01:25:07.077
<v JONES>Oh, I was young so I’d just feel the bed shaking and a couple of times I just thought I was having a bad dream.

1101
01:25:07.077 --> 01:25:13.077
But somebody would always call and check to see if everybody was alright.

1102
01:25:13.077 --> 01:25:21.385
Or the next day they'd say, "Well you know . . ." They'd name which plant it was had the explosion.

1103
01:25:21.385 --> 01:25:31.077
They'd say if they had noticed anything different at their home or how it had shaken their home during the night.

1104
01:25:31.077 --> 01:25:38.000
<v DRAGOON>And what do you remember about the relationship between the town and the refineries?

1105
01:25:38.000 --> 01:25:38.947
<v JONES>I don't remember.

1106
01:25:38.947 --> 01:25:39.263
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

1107
01:25:39.263 --> 01:25:39.579
<v JONES>Yeah.

1108
01:25:39.579 --> 01:25:42.105
<v DRAGOON>I know a lot of folks worked there . . .

1109
01:25:42.105 --> 01:25:42.421
<v JONES>Yeah.

1110
01:25:42.421 --> 01:25:43.053
<v DRAGOON>From Mossville.

1111
01:25:43.053 --> 01:25:50.000
Did you have a lot of . . . Do you have family members or friends that were working for the refineries growing up?

1112
01:25:50.000 --> 01:25:52.713
<v JONES>As I got older, yeah.

1113
01:25:52.713 --> 01:26:02.479
After I graduated there were some from the community that did work at one of the local industries.

1114
01:26:02.479 --> 01:26:11.160
If not Conoco it was maybe Olin, or the old Cities Service, which is now Citgo.

1115
01:26:11.160 --> 01:26:12.787
One of those.

1116
01:26:12.787 --> 01:26:18.755
I worked for a while myself at the old VCM plant.

1117
01:26:18.755 --> 01:26:20.926
<v DRAGOON>What was that like?

1118
01:26:20.926 --> 01:26:33.947
<v JONES>It was a learning experience because I worked as a receptionist and I did teletype work typing for the engineers, but that was it.

1119
01:26:33.947 --> 01:26:41.000
I didn't have to go out in the plants and do anything.

1120
01:26:41.000 --> 01:26:53.435
<v DRAGOON>I have now kind of a series of questions regarding the plants and I know because of your job it might be like a sensitive subject.

1121
01:26:53.435 --> 01:26:55.826
So feel free to answer . . .

1122
01:26:55.826 --> 01:26:56.304
<v JONES>Sure.

1123
01:26:56.304 --> 01:27:03.478
<v DRAGOON>What you feel comfortable with, or we can always restrict access to this interview later.

1124
01:27:03.478 --> 01:27:03.957
<v JONES>Okay.

1125
01:27:03.957 --> 01:27:07.304
<v DRAGOON>If you think back and aren't comfortable . . .

1126
01:27:07.304 --> 01:27:07.783
<v JONES>Okay.

1127
01:27:07.783 --> 01:27:09.696
<v DRAGOON>With something being released.

1128
01:27:09.696 --> 01:27:20.217
So, let’s see . . . So what do you remember . . . In 1980 Condea Vista announced that EDC had been leaked into the Mossville groundwater?

1129
01:27:20.217 --> 01:27:21.652
<v JONES>See I didn't . . .

1130
01:27:21.652 --> 01:27:25.000
<v DRAGOON>Do you remember much about that?

1131
01:27:25.000 --> 01:27:26.248
<v JONES>No I don't.

1132
01:27:26.248 --> 01:27:34.154
The first I remember about the EDC situation was in the nineties when the class action lawsuit came about.

1133
01:27:34.154 --> 01:27:37.483
I didn't know anything about it before then.

1134
01:27:37.483 --> 01:27:39.980
<v DRAGOON>Were you surprised to hear that?

1135
01:27:39.980 --> 01:27:41.228
<v JONES>Yes I was.

1136
01:27:41.228 --> 01:27:46.638
Yeah because like I said my mother was still living in the community,

1137
01:27:46.638 --> 01:27:54.128
my in-laws were still there, and they didn't know anything about it either until this lawsuit came about.

1138
01:27:54.128 --> 01:27:59.953
And that's around the time when the buyout came about in the late nineties,

1139
01:27:59.953 --> 01:28:06.195
like '98, because my mother lived there until that time, and so did my in-laws.

1140
01:28:06.195 --> 01:28:09.107
<v DRAGOON>And then their home was bought out?

1141
01:28:09.107 --> 01:28:10.356
<v JONES>They were . . . Yes.

1142
01:28:10.356 --> 01:28:15.765
They were bought out by the first . . . I call it the first buyout.

1143
01:28:15.765 --> 01:28:23.671
But I was just really surprised when they started talking about the leak because I knew nothing about it.

1144
01:28:23.671 --> 01:28:27.000
<v DRAGOON>Yeah what was your reaction to that?

1145
01:28:27.000 --> 01:28:31.033
<v JONES>I was just surprised when they start talking about it.

1146
01:28:31.033 --> 01:28:47.972
I guess a lot of things that went on a lot of people wouldn't talk to me about it because maybe they felt because I worked for the industry that I would be biased if they said something to me about it,

1147
01:28:47.972 --> 01:28:49.182
or to my

1148
01:28:49.182 --> 01:28:49.586
<c.p>mother.

1149
01:28:49.586 --> 01:28:56.039
So I think that's the reason I didn't hear a lot of what was going on.

1150
01:28:56.039 --> 01:29:00.878
<v DRAGOON>Do you know anything about the effort to clean up the area?

1151
01:29:00.878 --> 01:29:02.088
Did you hear . . .?

1152
01:29:02.088 --> 01:29:13.785
<v JONES>I remember that there was a big effort between the two companies Condea Vista . . . I think it was still Condea Vista at the time and maybe it was Sasol,

1153
01:29:13.785 --> 01:29:18.221
I can't really remember, and Conoco to get that cleaned up.

1154
01:29:18.221 --> 01:29:25.077
I remember there were a lot of meetings, and I kind of remember things from the news.

1155
01:29:25.077 --> 01:29:30.724
More of what I would see and hear on the local news than anything.

1156
01:29:30.724 --> 01:29:35.160
<v DRAGOON>I know that Bill Moyers did an investigative report on PBS.

1157
01:29:35.160 --> 01:29:36.773
Do you remember that?

1158
01:29:36.773 --> 01:29:40.000
Or the documentary Blue Vinyl at all?

1159
01:29:40.000 --> 01:29:54.000
<v JONES>No, I remember somebody telling me that I needed to try to find that on TV and watch it because they said it was so similar to what had happened with the community.

1160
01:29:54.000 --> 01:29:57.818
<v DRAGOON>And then . . . Okay, I'm backing up a little bit.

1161
01:29:57.818 --> 01:30:08.424
In 1997, I believe that's when they were doing blood tests and finding that Mossville residents had a higher level of dioxin in their blood?

1162
01:30:08.424 --> 01:30:13.515
<v JONES>I remember seeing that on the news and hearing about it, yes.

1163
01:30:13.515 --> 01:30:13.939
<v DRAGOON>Yeah.

1164
01:30:13.939 --> 01:30:19.030
What . . . Do you remember what people were talking about during that time?

1165
01:30:19.030 --> 01:30:20.303
Were people worried?

1166
01:30:20.303 --> 01:30:22.000
Were you worried?

1167
01:30:22.000 --> 01:30:23.860
<v JONES>No I wasn't worried.

1168
01:30:23.860 --> 01:30:35.488
I remember people saying that a lot of that had come from the EDC leak and that's what had made the dioxins into their system.

1169
01:30:35.488 --> 01:30:47.581
But my mother never was tested so I didn't really have a clear understanding as what all was being done on a lot of that, so.

1170
01:30:47.581 --> 01:30:48.977
<v DRAGOON>Did you . . . Yeah.

1171
01:30:48.977 --> 01:30:53.163
Did you feel like unsafe at all because of . . .?

1172
01:30:53.163 --> 01:30:54.558
<v JONES>No I didn't.

1173
01:30:54.558 --> 01:30:55.488
<v DRAGOON>Let's see.

1174
01:30:55.488 --> 01:30:59.209
Can you tell me more about the buyouts?

1175
01:30:59.209 --> 01:31:02.000
The first buyouts. [Coughs] Sorry.

1176
01:31:02.000 --> 01:31:12.284
<v JONES>All I can remember is that they got letters from Vista and they were saying that they were going to buy . . . Offering to buy them out.

1177
01:31:12.284 --> 01:31:18.613
They had in there what they were offering them as the purchase price for their homes.

1178
01:31:18.613 --> 01:31:26.524
And a lot of people, from what I can understand, they had a group that went to them and said,

1179
01:31:26.524 --> 01:31:32.062
"Okay if you sign this paper you're going to get this amount of money."

1180
01:31:32.062 --> 01:31:37.600
And they didn't really have a good understanding as to what a share was.

1181
01:31:37.600 --> 01:31:40.369
Because I think, if I'm not mistaken,

1182
01:31:40.369 --> 01:31:50.653
they were paid by the share and the people that had gotten them to sign up when they did find out what amount they were getting,

1183
01:31:50.653 --> 01:31:56.587
some were really disappointed because it was nowhere near what they thought they were getting.

1184
01:31:56.587 --> 01:32:02.520
<c.p>I remember telling my mother not to sign if she wasn't comfortable with doing it,

1185
01:32:02.520 --> 01:32:10.036
and she was really concerned that if she signed that might have some kind of effect on my job.

1186
01:32:10.036 --> 01:32:18.738
And I said, "Honey if they're going to let me go they're going to let me go whether you sign or not."

1187
01:32:18.738 --> 01:32:24.671
I said, "Just . . . I just don't want you to be left by yourself back here.

1188
01:32:24.671 --> 01:32:27.440
Everybody else is selling and moving out."

1189
01:32:27.440 --> 01:32:31.000
Because it was going to be really deserted.

1190
01:32:31.000 --> 01:32:33.033
So she kind of waited.

1191
01:32:33.033 --> 01:32:37.100
She didn't sign up when she got the first letter.

1192
01:32:37.100 --> 01:32:48.895
And I went with . . . my sister had come from Houston and she and I went to meetings so we could try to hear some of what they were saying.

1193
01:32:48.895 --> 01:32:59.876
Judge Gray was there and he was telling the people to sign up and they could not . . . Sasol did not have . . . I think he called it Sasol,

1194
01:32:59.876 --> 01:33:01.909
maybe it was Condea Vista.

1195
01:33:01.909 --> 01:33:08.823
Didn't have the right to force them I think is what he said for them to sign.

1196
01:33:08.823 --> 01:33:20.211
So people then started I think getting a little more comfortable about receiving the letters and not feeling that this is something they really really had to do.

1197
01:33:20.211 --> 01:33:28.344
So she eventually . . . My mother did eventually sign to move, but she was at the very last couple of days.

1198
01:33:28.344 --> 01:33:36.885
She barely got in before they shut down the buyout date, but a lot of them . . . They all spread it out.

1199
01:33:36.885 --> 01:33:40.139
They were not together like they were before.

1200
01:33:40.139 --> 01:33:43.392
But they kept in touch with each other.

1201
01:33:43.392 --> 01:33:51.120
They would talk to each other, but it was not the same little community that it had been before.

1202
01:33:51.120 --> 01:33:56.000
Some went to Westlake, some to Lake Charles, Sulphur . . . Different places.

1203
01:33:56.000 --> 01:33:58.341
<v DRAGOON>And what happened to the home?

1204
01:33:58.341 --> 01:33:59.511
Your childhood home.

1205
01:33:59.511 --> 01:34:01.852
<v JONES>You . . . They gave them an option.

1206
01:34:01.852 --> 01:34:04.192
You could sell the house yourself.

1207
01:34:04.192 --> 01:34:09.654
They would buy it, but then you could take the house and sell it,

1208
01:34:09.654 --> 01:34:16.286
but you had to have it off the property by the time they gave you the money.

1209
01:34:16.286 --> 01:34:20.967
So if you . . . The day you got your money, your check for

1210
01:34:20.967 --> 01:34:25.258
<c.p>your house and property, you had to be off the property.

1211
01:34:25.258 --> 01:34:26.819
You couldn't go back.

1212
01:34:26.819 --> 01:34:34.621
So some people were able to sell their homes and get a little extra money, and then others were not.

1213
01:34:34.621 --> 01:34:42.423
So if you didn't sell to someone else then they just went in and had somebody to tear it down.

1214
01:34:42.423 --> 01:34:51.005
So you had to try to get out everything that you needed and wanted out of there before you accepted that check.

1215
01:34:51.005 --> 01:34:56.467
Now they did pay additional monies for I think they called it water contamination.

1216
01:34:56.467 --> 01:35:00.758
I'm not too sure, but I think they did pay that.

1217
01:35:00.758 --> 01:35:03.879
So that was additional monies that they got.

1218
01:35:03.879 --> 01:35:07.000
<v DRAGOON>And that was . . . Who got that money?

1219
01:35:07.000 --> 01:35:07.837
<v JONES>The residents.

1220
01:35:07.837 --> 01:35:17.879
And like if you had lived, had been a resident of the community, but you had to have proof that you had lived there.

1221
01:35:17.879 --> 01:35:25.410
You had to have documentation for the number of years that you were claiming that you lived there.

1222
01:35:25.410 --> 01:35:36.707
So they gave you a list of things that you could have for your documentation as having lived there for the number of years that you said.

1223
01:35:36.707 --> 01:35:50.933
And you'd have to bring that with you when you would go to a meeting with I think it was . . . Yeah Flavin I believe was the company that was setting all of that up.

1224
01:35:50.933 --> 01:35:55.117
<v DRAGOON>Do you . . . Were folks getting sick from the water spills?

1225
01:35:55.117 --> 01:35:56.372
That you remember?

1226
01:35:56.372 --> 01:35:57.628
<v JONES>I don't remember.

1227
01:35:57.628 --> 01:36:03.485
Like I said by living in Lake Charles, and we didn't hear about it.

1228
01:36:03.485 --> 01:36:06.833
I mean you would hear people being sick,

1229
01:36:06.833 --> 01:36:13.946
but nobody ever said . . . And that was one of the things that I think that they said.

1230
01:36:13.946 --> 01:36:19.803
That people were getting sick and it was as a result of the water.

1231
01:36:19.803 --> 01:36:25.661
But at the time nobody really knew what had been making them sick, so.

1232
01:36:25.661 --> 01:36:29.008
<v DRAGOON>No one was talking about a possible connection?

1233
01:36:29.008 --> 01:36:31.937
<v JONES>Not when people first started getting sick,

1234
01:36:31.937 --> 01:36:43.234
but I think as the years went by that's when it seems like more and more people are saying that it was as a result of that.

1235
01:36:43.234 --> 01:36:47.000
<v DRAGOON>When did you notice people started getting sick?

1236
01:36:47.000 --> 01:36:51.282
<v JONES>I . . . Like I said, I’d just hear somebody had gotten sick.

1237
01:36:51.282 --> 01:36:57.121
Somebody maybe had cancer or something and they had died, or was in the hospital.

1238
01:36:57.121 --> 01:37:04.128
But nobody at that time could just really say, "Okay Brenda this is what they got sick from."

1239
01:37:04.128 --> 01:37:08.409
They just said so-and-so has cancer or they got heart trouble.

1240
01:37:08.409 --> 01:37:16.973
They're in and out the hospital and then the next thing you know they're bedridden and then they would say so-and-so died.

1241
01:37:16.973 --> 01:37:21.644
They died from this type of cancer, or that type of cancer.

1242
01:37:21.644 --> 01:37:34.490
Like I said, then in the late 90s into the early 2000s is when I started hearing more and more people saying that . . . Hearing rumblings that that was what it was caused from.

1243
01:37:34.490 --> 01:37:37.993
<v DRAGOON>Was that happening with mostly older folks or kids?

1244
01:37:37.993 --> 01:37:39.940
<v JONES>There were some young ones.

1245
01:37:39.940 --> 01:37:45.000
Some young people, and then some older people too that was dying.

1246
01:37:45.000 --> 01:37:48.429
<v DRAGOON>So it was both young people and old people . . .

1247
01:37:48.429 --> 01:37:48.810
<v JONES>Yeah.

1248
01:37:48.810 --> 01:37:51.095
<v DRAGOON>Who seemed to be getting sick?

1249
01:37:51.095 --> 01:37:51.476
<v JONES>Yeah.

1250
01:37:51.476 --> 01:37:58.333
And I think in the nineties is when the group MEAN [Mossville Environmental Action Now, Inc.] was formed.

1251
01:37:58.333 --> 01:38:00.238
The Mossville Environmental Action Network.

1252
01:38:00.238 --> 01:38:09.000
That's when they got together and started I believe putting together their information as to some of the things that was going on,

1253
01:38:09.000 --> 01:38:10.524
and maybe doing documentation.

1254
01:38:10.524 --> 01:38:17.000
I can't really say because I never did see it, but that's what I had heard.

1255
01:38:17.000 --> 01:38:21.622
<v DRAGOON>What else do you know about . . . Can you tell me more about MEAN?

1256
01:38:21.622 --> 01:38:23.400
What you know about them?

1257
01:38:23.400 --> 01:38:30.511
<v JONES>I just know they were active at one time in the nineties, but I don't know what happened to them.

1258
01:38:30.511 --> 01:38:40.111
I saw them a couple of years ago where there was like a small trailer located off of Prater Road that had a sign in the front.

1259
01:38:40.111 --> 01:38:47.933
So I was taking for granted that's where their office was located, but before then I don't know where they were located.

1260
01:38:47.933 --> 01:38:50.067
I don't know where they met.

1261
01:38:50.067 --> 01:38:56.111
Because you just didn't . . . Like at one time they were pretty active from what I can understand,

1262
01:38:56.111 --> 01:39:00.378
and then all at once it just kind of got real quiet.

1263
01:39:00.378 --> 01:39:04.289
Then I think when Doctor Gupta came . . . Is it Sanjay Gupta?

1264
01:39:04.289 --> 01:39:06.778
I'm not sure what the name is,

1265
01:39:06.778 --> 01:39:15.311
but I want to think somebody said that MEAN and the group from Carlyss had gotten together and they were working together with him.

1266
01:39:15.311 --> 01:39:25.267
So I don't really know because by that time my mother wasn't living over there and I didn't hear much anymore as to actually what was going on.

1267
01:39:25.267 --> 01:39:28.111
I’d hear little bits and pieces, but not

1268
01:39:28.111 --> 01:39:33.444
<c.p>enough to say that I would have a full story as to what was what.

1269
01:39:33.444 --> 01:39:37.000
<v DRAGOON>Do you know if they were mostly Mossville residents?

1270
01:39:37.000 --> 01:39:37.388
<v JONES>Yes.

1271
01:39:37.388 --> 01:39:40.108
With MEAN, it was mostly Mossville residents.

1272
01:39:40.108 --> 01:39:43.604
<v DRAGOON>And they were well supported by the Mossville community?

1273
01:39:43.604 --> 01:39:52.540
<v JONES>Now that I don't know because I could hear that they were a group, but I never would hear anybody like my in-laws,

1274
01:39:52.540 --> 01:39:59.921
the siblings, my husband's siblings that still lived there . . . I never heard them say they went to a meeting.

1275
01:39:59.921 --> 01:40:06.137
I didn't hear any of the younger people saying that they were going to a meeting.

1276
01:40:06.137 --> 01:40:11.187
And a lot of the older people, they weren't going to get involved.

1277
01:40:11.187 --> 01:40:25.950
They were like they had their health issues and they were mainly interested in trying to stay stress free and not have any more strain than they really needed with their health conditions if they did have one.

1278
01:40:25.950 --> 01:40:29.835
<v DRAGOON>Do you know what the mission of MEAN was exactly?

1279
01:40:29.835 --> 01:40:30.223
<v JONES>No.

1280
01:40:30.223 --> 01:40:31.000
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

1281
01:40:31.000 --> 01:40:35.772
<v JONES>No and like I said, by me working for the company I was going to be

1282
01:40:35.772 --> 01:40:37.561
<c.p>treated with a long handled spoon.

1283
01:40:37.561 --> 01:40:41.140
They . . . I wasn't going to get a whole whole lot of information.

1284
01:40:41.140 --> 01:40:45.018
<v DRAGOON>Sounds like you were kind of in the middle of all of this.

1285
01:40:45.018 --> 01:40:45.614
<v JONES>Yeah [laughs].

1286
01:40:45.614 --> 01:40:45.912
Yeah.

1287
01:40:45.912 --> 01:40:48.000
<v DRAGOON>What was that like for you?

1288
01:40:48.000 --> 01:40:49.611
<v JONES>It didn't bother me.

1289
01:40:49.611 --> 01:40:53.236
I knew what my main concern was, my mom.

1290
01:40:53.236 --> 01:40:56.458
Trying to make sure that she was okay.

1291
01:40:56.458 --> 01:41:00.083
Then my in-laws, making sure that they were okay.

1292
01:41:00.083 --> 01:41:06.125
The rest it didn't matter if I didn't have any communications with them or not.

1293
01:41:06.125 --> 01:41:08.542
<v DRAGOON>Was your job difficult for you?

1294
01:41:08.542 --> 01:41:10.153
<v JONES>No. No it wasn't.

1295
01:41:10.153 --> 01:41:20.625
Not when it came to that because I mean I had gone at one time to a meeting and that's when I first heard about MEAN.

1296
01:41:20.625 --> 01:41:23.042
I didn't know anything about it.

1297
01:41:23.042 --> 01:41:29.889
I saw people there that I knew that were members of it, but they spoke to me.

1298
01:41:29.889 --> 01:41:36.736
We hugged each other and everything, but they never treated me any different than they had before.

1299
01:41:36.736 --> 01:41:46.000
So I felt it was okay as long as we weren't having to battle it out every time we saw each other.

1300
01:41:46.000 --> 01:41:47.850
<v DRAGOON>Sounds like an incredibly complicated situation.

1301
01:41:47.850 --> 01:41:48.158
<v JONES>[Laughs].

1302
01:41:48.158 --> 01:41:49.392
It could have been.

1303
01:41:49.392 --> 01:41:49.700
Yes.

1304
01:41:49.700 --> 01:41:53.092
It could have been a lot worse than what it was.

1305
01:41:53.092 --> 01:41:56.483
But like I said I don't know when they got started.

1306
01:41:56.483 --> 01:41:58.333
I don't know who organized it.

1307
01:41:58.333 --> 01:42:02.033
And like I said, I just hadn't heard anything in so long.

1308
01:42:02.033 --> 01:42:10.975
One day I was on my lunch break and I rode by and I saw that sign and I was like . . . I wasn't sure I had seen it correct.

1309
01:42:10.975 --> 01:42:15.908
So I had to turn around and go back and sure enough that's where it was.

1310
01:42:15.908 --> 01:42:19.300
So I just didn't hear anybody say they were meeting anymore.

1311
01:42:19.300 --> 01:42:23.000
<v DRAGOON>So it seems like they kind of disappeared a little bit?

1312
01:42:23.000 --> 01:42:23.904
<v JONES>Maybe so.

1313
01:42:23.904 --> 01:42:24.356
Yeah.

1314
01:42:24.356 --> 01:42:29.329
Or maybe they had a new person that was in charge.

1315
01:42:29.329 --> 01:42:30.685
I don't know.

1316
01:42:30.685 --> 01:42:32.945
<v DRAGOON>How were Mossville residents organizing?

1317
01:42:32.945 --> 01:42:39.726
Or what was the . . . What were people talking about during the time of the buyouts?

1318
01:42:39.726 --> 01:42:42.438
What was that like that time?

1319
01:42:42.438 --> 01:42:46.055
<v JONES>Just wondering where they were going to move.

1320
01:42:46.055 --> 01:42:47.411
The first buyout?

1321
01:42:47.411 --> 01:42:47.863
<v DRAGOON>Yeah.

1322
01:42:47.863 --> 01:42:49.671
<v JONES>That was the thing.

1323
01:42:49.671 --> 01:43:03.233
Just wondering where they were going to move and hoping that they would get enough money to get a home where they were going to have the home paid for,

1324
01:43:03.233 --> 01:43:06.397
and not end up with a note.

1325
01:43:06.397 --> 01:43:13.630
Because they were older people . . . I don't care what the home looked like to somebody else,

1326
01:43:13.630 --> 01:43:19.959
but to these people, what they had if it was paid for, they could

1327
01:43:19.959 --> 01:43:27.644
<c.p>handle making the notes for insurance, or . . . homeowner insurance, fire insurance, and then their other living needs.

1328
01:43:27.644 --> 01:43:38.041
But you wouldn't want to be at that age thinking about you’re having to buy a new home and you're just starting out.

1329
01:43:38.041 --> 01:43:43.466
You don't have enough to have it paid for when you move.

1330
01:43:43.466 --> 01:43:50.247
I think that was more the concern that people were having in regards to it,

1331
01:43:50.247 --> 01:43:55.671
and trying to handle the money properly when they did get it.

1332
01:43:55.671 --> 01:44:02.000
<v DRAGOON>Do you know if there were any education, or training programs for residents?

1333
01:44:02.000 --> 01:44:05.199
<v JONES>Yeah back in the mid-nineties . . . Let's see.

1334
01:44:05.199 --> 01:44:06.570
It was Conoco.

1335
01:44:06.570 --> 01:44:11.141
I think it was just Conoco Inc. at the time.

1336
01:44:11.141 --> 01:44:17.082
They were looking at buying some properties and some of the residents said,

1337
01:44:17.082 --> 01:44:26.223
"Well you all are looking at expanding in this area but we don't have many people working at your company."

1338
01:44:26.223 --> 01:44:33.993
So that's when there were some meetings with the company and the residents at the Rigmaiden Center.

1339
01:44:33.993 --> 01:44:41.306
They started talking and trying to figure out where it would be a win-win for both.

1340
01:44:41.306 --> 01:44:49.990
And that's when they said, "Okay to get a job at the plant you have to have certain training."

1341
01:44:49.990 --> 01:45:10.557
So that's when they talked to ABC School and had them to have brochures put out with the different training classes that they had where people could go so that they could be trained to get out and get a job as a pipe fitter,

1342
01:45:10.557 --> 01:45:21.526
or a welder, because a lot of people were interested in working at the plants but they said they just didn't have the training.

1343
01:45:21.526 --> 01:45:30.210
So this was an opportunity where they could get some training to get qualified to get into those positions.

1344
01:45:30.210 --> 01:45:39.808
So there were people that went, but I think after a while some of them went for a couple of years.

1345
01:45:39.808 --> 01:45:46.663
And you had to have been a resident of Mossville to get into the school.

1346
01:45:46.663 --> 01:45:51.234
Then the companies had offered to pay for that training.

1347
01:45:51.234 --> 01:45:54.890
So there were people that went, but they

1348
01:45:54.890 --> 01:45:59.003
<c.p>ended up dropping out for one reason or another.

1349
01:45:59.003 --> 01:46:06.773
A lot of times the jobs they had, they couldn't do the training and work their jobs.

1350
01:46:06.773 --> 01:46:08.144
The hours interfered.

1351
01:46:08.144 --> 01:46:15.000
<v DRAGOON>What do you remember about toxic chemicals being dumped at the Willow Springs facility?

1352
01:46:15.000 --> 01:46:27.200
<v JONES>Yeah that was something that was done by BFI and Cesco because there ended up being a class action I think lawsuit on that.

1353
01:46:27.200 --> 01:46:37.875
A lot of people that lived on High Hope Road, those residents had filed suits against those two companies about that.

1354
01:46:37.875 --> 01:46:44.483
I remember seeing a lot on TV and reading newspaper articles on those.

1355
01:46:44.483 --> 01:46:53.633
Some of them had even gone to Washington to talk about some of the issues that were there.

1356
01:46:53.633 --> 01:46:58.208
They complained about the odors coming from the pit.

1357
01:46:58.208 --> 01:47:07.358
And monitoring wells were set up on different properties so that they could see what the readings were.

1358
01:47:07.358 --> 01:47:16.000
<v DRAGOON>Do you remember what the reactions of residents were during that time and what was happening?

1359
01:47:16.000 --> 01:47:26.913
<v JONES>My grandmother lived really close because the road that would go to the BFI dumpsite was . . . It was very near her property;

1360
01:47:26.913 --> 01:47:29.394
the border of her property.

1361
01:47:29.394 --> 01:47:38.323
They would have smells, but a monitoring well was never put on that . . . Of my family’s property there.

1362
01:47:38.323 --> 01:47:48.244
I don't know if it was anybody that was really close to the pit that had the monitoring wells done.

1363
01:47:48.244 --> 01:48:04.118
I think it was the people that were further down the road on High Hope that had the wells put in and that's where the majority of the complaints were coming from.

1364
01:48:04.118 --> 01:48:14.039
<v DRAGOON>Let's see . . . I know in the nineties Mossville residents were organizing to address gas flares and illnesses caused by emissions.

1365
01:48:14.039 --> 01:48:17.016
Do you remember anything about that?

1366
01:48:17.016 --> 01:48:23.961
<v JONES>I remember hearing something about that, but I wasn't involved with any of it . . .

1367
01:48:23.961 --> 01:48:24.457
<v DRAGOON>Right.

1368
01:48:24.457 --> 01:48:27.929
<v JONES>With the company nor with the community.

1369
01:48:27.929 --> 01:48:35.866
Yeah but I can remember them saying with the flaring they could see the bright light.

1370
01:48:35.866 --> 01:48:44.299
And I can remember that . . . Seeing that as a child also because I remember we'd be outside.

1371
01:48:44.299 --> 01:48:55.213
We didn't have street lights, but when the flares would go real bright we could run outside and play a little longer.

1372
01:48:55.213 --> 01:49:10.094
But it's strange and it's sad that we were little out there running and playing and we never thought about it would be anything that could have made us sick.

1373
01:49:10.094 --> 01:49:22.000
But then like I said years later people started doing more research and finding out more about this . . . These type of things, so.

1374
01:49:22.000 --> 01:49:26.632
<v DRAGOON>It just wasn't talked about or really known?

1375
01:49:26.632 --> 01:49:33.579
<v JONES>No. Nobody ever . . . like I said we . . . My mother and her friends,

1376
01:49:33.579 --> 01:49:39.947
they just would talk about how bright the flare would get.

1377
01:49:39.947 --> 01:49:48.053
But . . . And if you were passing by you could feel the heat from it,

1378
01:49:48.053 --> 01:49:59.053
but to say that they’re . . . I never hear anybody say that it had made them sick at that time.

1379
01:49:59.053 --> 01:50:06.579
I don't know about years later if they changed about it or not.

1380
01:50:06.579 --> 01:50:08.895
Their thinking on it.

1381
01:50:08.895 --> 01:50:15.842
<v DRAGOON>Do you remember PPG and Vista publishing a newsletter called The Source?

1382
01:50:15.842 --> 01:50:19.316
Do you know anything about that?

1383
01:50:19.316 --> 01:50:19.895
<v JONES>No.

1384
01:50:19.895 --> 01:50:20.474
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

1385
01:50:20.474 --> 01:50:23.368
Let's see . . . where we're at.

1386
01:50:23.368 --> 01:50:29.158
We’re almost . . . It looks like we're almost at two hours.

1387
01:50:29.158 --> 01:50:30.316
Time flies.

1388
01:50:30.316 --> 01:50:30.895
<v JONES>Yes.

1389
01:50:30.895 --> 01:50:37.842
<v DRAGOON>So just wanted to ask you some general Mossville wrapping up questions.

1390
01:50:37.842 --> 01:50:39.000
<v JONES>Okay.

1391
01:50:39.000 --> 01:50:42.571
<v DRAGOON>What are some of your fondest memories of Mossville?

1392
01:50:42.571 --> 01:50:44.159
<v JONES>Just being with everybody.

1393
01:50:44.159 --> 01:50:46.143
Just how relaxed everybody seemed.

1394
01:50:46.143 --> 01:50:50.508
Where you always knew you had a safe place to go.

1395
01:50:50.508 --> 01:51:00.825
If you needed to . . . If you got home and you couldn't get in at your house because the doors were locked for some reason or another,

1396
01:51:00.825 --> 01:51:07.968
you could always go to one of the neighbors and they were going to always see about you.

1397
01:51:07.968 --> 01:51:19.079
It was . . . You didn't have to worry about “latch key kids” back then because it was always somebody that was at home to look out for your child,

1398
01:51:19.079 --> 01:51:20.270
or your children.

1399
01:51:20.270 --> 01:51:30.190
If somebody had to go to the doctor and they didn't have a car, somebody always provided transportation and it wasn't one of these things

1400
01:51:30.190 --> 01:51:34.952
<c.p>where you got to pay me to take you here or there.

1401
01:51:34.952 --> 01:51:42.095
It's kind of like it's my car and if it's my car it's your car if it's needed.

1402
01:51:42.095 --> 01:51:52.016
If somebody needed something to cook and they didn't have it at home, you always could go to a neighbor and you could get it.

1403
01:51:52.016 --> 01:51:54.794
Everybody lived really close to each other.

1404
01:51:54.794 --> 01:51:56.381
The houses were close.

1405
01:51:56.381 --> 01:52:06.698
You could just go outside and stick your arm over and touch your neighbor's arm if they were in their yard at the fence or something.

1406
01:52:06.698 --> 01:52:09.873
It was just a little tight knit community.

1407
01:52:09.873 --> 01:52:12.254
It’s what I liked about it.

1408
01:52:12.254 --> 01:52:19.000
Like I said everybody just being there to support each other when something was going on.

1409
01:52:19.000 --> 01:52:21.917
<v DRAGOON>What will you miss most about Mossville?

1410
01:52:21.917 --> 01:52:31.917
<v JONES>Just seeing . . . Knowing that I can ride through there whenever I wanted to and just look at the school and show my kids like,

1411
01:52:31.917 --> 01:52:41.083
"Oh this is where I went to . . . started out my . . . all the way first grade through my freshman year of high school.

1412
01:52:41.083 --> 01:52:44.417
This is where we had a football field."

1413
01:52:44.417 --> 01:52:48.583
And how close it was to where I grew up.

1414
01:52:48.583 --> 01:52:56.500
But now when I ride through there, you can’t even tell that the area we lived in ever existed.

1415
01:52:56.500 --> 01:53:00.250
It's just all . . . All the trees and everything's down.

1416
01:53:00.250 --> 01:53:07.750
I can remember as a child too, we would go pick blackberries along the side of the road,

1417
01:53:07.750 --> 01:53:14.000
but now all of that's gone once they came through and bulldozed everything over.

1418
01:53:14.000 --> 01:53:24.073
I remember riding through the neighborhood, I guess about four years ago, and it was really hard to tell where I had actually grown up.

1419
01:53:24.073 --> 01:53:32.937
I knew I lived on a corner and it just so happened that the guy across the street on the next corner,

1420
01:53:32.937 --> 01:53:37.772
he still had his horse pen up where he kept his horses.

1421
01:53:37.772 --> 01:53:43.816
So I knew about where I was, but everything . . . It just grew up so fast.

1422
01:53:43.816 --> 01:53:49.053
The trees and all, but like I said now all of that's gone.

1423
01:53:49.053 --> 01:53:58.723
You just ride on the Old Spanish Trail and you look back towards behind the school and all you see is mounds of dirt.

1424
01:53:58.723 --> 01:54:00.738
Whereas when I'm riding on

1425
01:54:00.738 --> 01:54:09.602
<c.p>Houston River Road on Sundays to go to church, I can look almost from Houston River Road back to Old Spanish Trail.

1426
01:54:09.602 --> 01:54:12.825
It's no trees or anything blocking the view.

1427
01:54:12.825 --> 01:54:19.675
It's just . . . Now you're just getting to see equipment and fences and units beginning to go up.

1428
01:54:19.675 --> 01:54:22.092
So it's a different look now.

1429
01:54:22.092 --> 01:54:25.718
You just never knew it was ever a community.

1430
01:54:25.718 --> 01:54:33.777
It's almost just like it was all industry and it seems like it's been like that now for a while.

1431
01:54:33.777 --> 01:54:37.000
<v DRAGOON>And what does Mossville mean to you?

1432
01:54:37.000 --> 01:54:37.397
<v JONES>Home.

1433
01:54:37.397 --> 01:54:39.384
My old . . . My first home.

1434
01:54:39.384 --> 01:54:45.344
A place where I grew up and I lived until the time I got married.

1435
01:54:45.344 --> 01:54:47.331
And it will always be.

1436
01:54:47.331 --> 01:54:52.099
I mean it . . . I may not have the house to look at,

1437
01:54:52.099 --> 01:54:57.662
but I'll still have my memories and the people that I grew up with.

1438
01:54:57.662 --> 01:55:00.444
And it's always good to see them.

1439
01:55:00.444 --> 01:55:08.391
If I'm at a Mardi Gras ball or something and I'll see somebody I hadn't seen in a long time,

1440
01:55:08.391 --> 01:55:11.172
we always got to talk about Mossville.

1441
01:55:11.172 --> 01:55:15.543
What we . . . Get that last little talk in there on it.

1442
01:55:15.543 --> 01:55:17.132
So we'll miss it,

1443
01:55:17.132 --> 01:55:26.669
and I think my kids will too because they stayed every summer with my mother there until they got older and had different activities,

1444
01:55:26.669 --> 01:55:30.245
but they did know what Mossville was about, too.

1445
01:55:30.245 --> 01:55:33.026
And they have fond memories of it.

1446
01:55:33.026 --> 01:55:37.000
<v DRAGOON>Do you have anything else you'd like to add?

1447
01:55:37.000 --> 01:55:37.667
<v JONES>That's it.

1448
01:55:37.667 --> 01:55:40.667
That's all I can think of at this time.

1449
01:55:40.667 --> 01:55:44.000
<v DRAGOON>Thank you so much for sitting down with us today.

1450
01:55:44.000 --> 01:55:44.333
<v JONES>Sure.

1451
01:55:44.333 --> 01:55:45.000
No problem.

1452
01:55:45.000 --> 01:55:46.333
<v DRAGOON>And telling your stories.

1453
01:55:46.333 --> 01:55:47.000
<v JONES>Enjoyed it.

1454
01:55:47.000 --> 01:55:47.667
<v DRAGOON>Thank you.

1455
01:55:47.667 --> 01:55:50.000
<v JONES>Glad I got the opportunity ladies.

1456
01:55:50.000 --> 01:55:50.200
<c.p>[End Tape 4622.

1457
01:55:50.000 --> 01:55:50.200
End Session I.]
